hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"15731","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Muslim informed me, he said: Tawus narrated to me, from Aisha, that she said: Allah and His Messenger are the mawla of the one who has no mawla, and the maternal uncle is the heir of the one who has no heir."
"15732","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim said to me, from Umar, Ali, Ibn Masud, Masruq, al-Nakhai and al-Shabi, that when a man dies leaving his mawalis who freed him, and leaving no kin except a mother or a maternal aunt, they gave his inheritance to her and did not give his mawalis inheritance along with her; and they do not give his mawalis inheritance along with any kin."
"15733","From Hushaym, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: It was said to him: Abu Ubayda ibn Abdullah gave a sister the whole wealth. Al-Shabi said: One better than Abu Ubayda did that: Abdullah ibn Masud used to do that."
"15734","From Hushaym, from Ismail ibn Salim, he said: I witnessed al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman when a dispute was brought to him concerning a boy who died leaving his mother and his mawalis who had freed him. The dispute over his inheritance was brought to al-Qasim, and he said: You carried him in your belly and suckled him with your breast; the whole wealth is yours."
"15735","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, from Abu Habib al-Iraqi, that a woman had a son who died leaving fifty dinars, with no heir except his mother, and his mawalis far from him. Abu al-Shatha said to her: Woe to you, take it and give them nothing."
"15736","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, that Zayd ibn Thabit used to give inheritance to the mawalis to the exclusion of the kin through women."
"15737","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, that he used to give inheritance to the mawalis to the exclusion of the kin through women."
"15738","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Mughira, from al-Shabi, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit never returned anything to the kin through women at all."
"15739","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, he said: I came upon Abdullah ibn Shaddad while he was narrating to the people, and I heard him saying at the end of the report: My sister. He said: I asked the people, and his companions narrated to me that he narrated to them that a daughter of Hamza, who was a sister of Abdullah through his mother, had a mawla die leaving his daughter and leaving the daughter of Hamza. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided like that. From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, the like of it."
"15740","Al-Thawri said: Mansur and al-Amash informed me that Ibrahim, when the daughter of Hamza was mentioned to him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only gave her a grant. One of the jurists said to him: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her a grant, then we give as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave."
"15741","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Husayn, he said: I disputed before Shurayh concerning a mukatab of mine who left children, with a remainder of his contract upon him. Shurayh gave me what remained upon him of his contract, and gave his two daughters two-thirds, and made Abu Husayn an asaba, so he gave him as inheritance what remained."
"15742","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, he said: A man wished to buy a slave, but no sale was concluded between him and his owner. A Muslim man swore by his freeing. He bought him and freed him, and mentioned it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: How about his companionship? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is yours, unless he has an asaba; if he has no asaba, he is yours."
"15743","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, concerning a woman who bought her father and freed him, then her father died leaving his two daughters, one of them the one who freed him. He said: They inherit from him by the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, two-thirds, and what remains is for the one who freed him."
"15744","From Ibn Tawus, he said: He was asked about a man who died leaving his mother a slave woman, and leaving no heir. He said: She is bought from his wealth, then freed, and she inherits him. Mamar said: It reached me from Ibn Masud the like of it."
"15745","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man contracted a man in manumission and settled with him, and his master did not stipulate that your wala is mine; whose is his wala? He said: His master's. Amr ibn Dinar said it. I said to Ata: A mukatab who contracted and stipulated that my wala is to whom I wish; is it allowed? He said: Yes. Ata and Amr ibn Dinar said: The Muslims are bound by their conditions. It was said to him: The mukatab died after fulfilling his contract, without assigning his wala to anyone, leaving wealth? He said: It belongs to the one who contracted him. Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"15746","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If he stipulates in his contract that I shall take as mawla whom I wish, it is allowed."
"15747","And I know Mamar only informed us, from Qatada, that he said: When the mukatab pays and pays all his contract, he takes as mawla whom he wishes. Mamar said: I did not see the people follow him in that."
"15748","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if a people pay blood-money on his behalf but did not take him as mawla? He said: He has taken them as mawla when they paid blood-money on his behalf; can that be except by alliance? I said: What if a people became angry for him and protected him, but did not pay blood-money on his behalf and did not take him as mawla? He said: His wala belongs to the one who contracted him; he is most entitled to his inheritance. Amr ibn Dinar said it to me. I said to Ata: Where is the word of Umar: His inheritance is for whoever became angry for him, or protected him, or aided him? He said: This is not like the one who has no mawla; this one, his mawla is known."
"15749","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man contracting a slave of his in manumission, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stipulate his wala. He said: Qatada used to say: If he did not stipulate his wala, he takes as mawla whom he wishes when he is freed. Mamar said: The people reject that from him."
"15750","From Mamar, from Qatada, that a man came to Ibn Masud and said: A mawla of mine died whom I freed as saiba, leaving wealth. He said: You are most entitled to his wealth. He said: I freed him only for Allah. He said: You are most entitled to his wealth; if you leave it, show it to me; there are many heirs here. Meaning the treasury."
"15751","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: A man came to Abdullah ibn Masud and said to him: I had a slave and I freed him, making him a saiba in the path of Allah. Abdullah said to him: The people of Islam do not set free as saiba; only the people of pre-Islamic ignorance used to set free as saiba. You are the nearest of people to his favor, and the most entitled of people to his inheritance. If you are scrupulous about anything, show it to us, and he put it in the treasury. From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn Masud, the like of it."
"15752","Mamar informed us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The saiba is inherited by his mawla who freed him, and he inherits from him."
"15753","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me that Tariq, the mawla of Ibn Abi Alqama, bought a household traveling to Syria and freed them. They returned to Yemen. I said: Did he set them free as saiba, or free them outright? He said: He set them free as saiba. He said: They died leaving sixteen thousand dirhams or seventeen thousand. He wrote to Tariq, but he refused to take their inheritance. Yala wrote concerning that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar wrote to Yala: Offer it to Tariq; if he refuses, seek with it slaves and free them."
"15754","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa saying: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning a saiba who died without taking anyone as mawla, that his inheritance is for the believers, and that they all pay blood-money on his behalf. Sulayman ibn Musa said: The saiba gifts his wala to whom he wishes; if he does not, his wala is for all the believers; the imam pays blood-money on his behalf and inherits him."
"15755","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, concerning a man who freed a slave as saiba, and what is the sunna concerning it? He said: His master has nothing to do with him; the Muslims inherit him from him and pay blood-money on his behalf."
"15756","From Mamar, from al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The saiba and the charity are for their day. Meaning the Day of Resurrection."
"15757","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Ammar, that Ibn Umar freed a saiba and inherited from them dinars, and put them into slaves to be freed. From Mamar, from al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, from Ibn Umar, the like of that. Al-Thawri said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed me of it."
"15758","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Salim the mawla of Abu Hudhayfa was freed by a woman of the Ansar. When he was killed on the day of Yamama, his inheritance was given to the Ansari woman who had freed him, or to her son."
"15759","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Amir al-Shabi, that Salim the mawla of Abu Hudhayfa was freed by a woman of the Ansar. When he was killed, Umar called her to his inheritance, but she refused to accept it and said: I freed him only as a saiba for Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"15760","From Ismail, he said: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to me, he said: I said to al-Shabi: Abu al-Aliya bequeathed all his wealth, and he had freed a saiba. Al-Shabi said: That is not his to do."
"15761","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about a man who frees his slave as saiba: May he assign his wala to whom he wishes? He said: His master has nothing to do with him; the ruler inherits him and pays blood-money on his behalf."
"15762","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: We set a slave free as saiba; may he take as mawla whom he wishes? He said: Yes; it used to be said: He takes as mawla whom he wishes, unless he says with that: I am quit of your wala and your liability; then he takes as mawla whom he wishes. I reviewed it with Ata, and he said: We knew that when he says: You are free, a saiba, then he takes as mawla whom he wishes, and he is set free, even if he did not say: Take as mawla whom you wish, when he says: You are a saiba. I said to Ata: What distinguishes his saying: You are free, from his saying: You are a saiba? He said: He set him free as saiba; he released him, he let him go. I said to Ata: The saiba did not take anyone as mawla until he died? He said: The one who freed him is called to his inheritance; if he accepts it, he is most entitled to it; otherwise slaves are bought with it and freed. Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I see only that. I said to Ata: The one who freed him, is he then held by a vow for what he took? He said: Yes. I said to him: Where is the letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz concerning that: His inheritance is for the believers? He refused but that the one who freed him be called to his inheritance. I said to him: He had counted it as reward; how does he return to something for Allah? He said: What about one who frees for Allah and then takes his inheritance?"
"15763","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Ubayd ibn Abi al-Jad, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, he said: Salim the mawla of Abu Hudhayfa was killed on the day of Yamama leaving inheritance. His inheritance was taken to the asaba of a woman of the Ansar called Amra, who had freed him. They said: He was a saiba, and refused to take it. Umar said: Keep it for his mother until she completes it, or dies."
"15764","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that Ali, Umar and Zayd ibn Thabit used to assign wala to the senior. Sufyan said: Its explanation is: A man died leaving his two sons and leaving mawalis. Then one of the two sons died leaving male children. Wala passed to their uncle. Then the uncle died later, leaving five children, and the first had seven. They said: Wala is upon twelve shares, as if the grandfather is the one who died, so they gave him as inheritance."
"15765","From Mamar, from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, that Ali and Zayd ibn Thabit judged concerning a man who left his full brother and his paternal brother, and left a mawla: They gave wala to his full brother to the exclusion of his paternal brother. They said: If the full brother dies, wala returns to the paternal brother. They said: If the paternal brother dies leaving sons, wala returns to the sons of the full brother, if he has sons. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said the like of that. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the like of that."
"15766","Mamar said: I said to Ibn Tawus: What if one has ten and one has one; is it halves? He said: My father used to say: It is among them upon eleven shares in wala. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, the like of it."
"15767","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man died leaving his two sons, and leaving mawalis. Then one of his two sons died leaving men, and some of the mawalis of their father died. He said: The most entitled of people to the emancipator on that day inherits him. I said: From whom is this? He said: We found the people upon it."
"15768","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he used to say: If a man dies having a mawla, and the deceased has sons, then one of his full sons dies having a male child, then the mawla dies, his inheritance belongs to his full sons, and the grandsons get nothing."
"15769","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr inherited Aisha the Mother of the Believers, and Abd al-Rahman had died before her. Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman inherited Aisha, then Abdullah died leaving his two sons. Dhakwan the mawla of Aisha died, while al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr was alive. Ibn al-Zubayr gave Dhakwan as inheritance to the two sons of Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, leaving al-Qasim, though al-Qasim was more entitled than they. Ata said: That was held against Ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Qasim kept speaking about it, saying: What precedent did he follow in that?"
"15770","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulayka, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad disputed before Ibn al-Zubayr concerning a mawla of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her. The sons of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr disputed him. Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad was nearer to Aisha, and Abd al-Rahman was the full brother of Aisha. Ibn al-Zubayr judged with it for the sons of Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, though they were farther by a father. Ibn Abi Mulayka said: Ibn al-Zubayr feared for her... Ibn Abi Mulayka said: When Abd al-Malik came, it was said to al-Qasim: Dispute, for you will attain. Al-Qasim said: I disputed on that day; had I been given anything I would have taken. As for today, I shall not dispute."
"15771","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: My father informed me that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan used to transfer wala."
"15772","From Ibn Jurayj, that Amr ibn Shuayb mentioned that with them is a letter from Umar ibn al-Khattab to Amr ibn al-As: If a man has mawalis and has two sons, and the father dies, wala belongs to his two sons. Then one of his two sons dies having male children, then some of the mawalis die: The grandson is upon his father's share of wala, and wala does not belong to his uncle."
"15773","He said: Amr ibn Shuayb mentioned that Umar ibn al-Khattab placed wala in the position of wealth; he does not transfer it."
"15774","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shubruma mentioning that Ali, Abdullah ibn Masud and Zayd ibn Thabit judged that wala is transferred as lineage is transferred; the one who inherited the bestower of the favor does not retain it, but it is transferred to the nearest of people to the bestower of the favor."
"15775","From al-Thawri, he said: When the son of the woman dies, and her male grandchildren, wala returns to the asaba, the asaba of the woman."
"15776","And Mughira informed me, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, that he used to say: It follows the course of wealth; it does not return. And the first view is dearer to Sufyan."
"15777","From al-Thawri, concerning two men who freed a slave: One of them died leaving male children. He said: Wala belongs to his children along with their uncle, between them halves."
"15778","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man freed by a woman: His wala belongs to her children, so long as a male remains among them; when they pass away, wala belongs to the asaba of their mother. It reached me that Qatada mentioned from Khilas ibn Umar ibn Ali. Mamar said: Qatada said: Al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib said: Wala belongs to their sons. Ibn Jurayj said it."
"15779","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ali and al-Zubayr disputed concerning a mawla of Safiyya. Umar judged blood-money upon Ali, and inheritance for al-Zubayr."
"15780","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, he said: When the woman dies leaving mawalis, inheritance belongs to her children, and blood-money is upon them. He said: Ibn Abi Layla used to judge with it."
"15781","From al-Thawri, he said: Concerning a woman who died leaving her father, her son, and leaving mawalis. He said: Mughira informed me, from Ibrahim, he said: The father has a sixth of wala, and the rest is for the son. Al-Hakam and Hammad said: Wala belongs to the son. He said: It reached me from Zayd ibn Thabit that he said: Wala belongs to the son."
"15782","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The woman with male children: Who pays blood-money on her behalf? He said: Her asaba. I said: And her male children inherit her? He said: Yes."
"15783","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A slave buys himself from his master; may he take as mawla whom he wishes? He said: His wala belongs to his master. Had his master wished, he would not have allowed that sale, and that slave whom he took from him belongs to his master. I said to him: The slave, did he buy himself with the slave's wealth? He said: Yes, it is his master's wealth. I said: His master knew he was buying himself? He said: He is now settling; and his wala belongs to his master. Ibn Jurayj said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa al-Shami saying: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Any slave who buys himself with wealth belonging to his mawla belongs to his mawla."
"15784","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A free man married a slave girl of mine; she conceived, and I freed her child in her womb; whose is his wala? He said: His wala belongs to the one who freed him, but his inheritance is for his father."
"15785","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, they said: Women do not inherit of wala except what they freed, or what those they freed freed. Others than they said: Or what those they freed drew in; otherwise it excludes them."
"15786","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, he said: Women do not inherit of wala except what they freed, or contracted in manumission."
"15787","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: Women do not inherit of wala except what they contracted in manumission, or freed. Al-Hakam said: Ibrahim informed me, from Ibn Masud, the like of it. Al-Hakam said: Shurayh used to say it."
"15788","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: The woman inherits nothing of wala unless she frees him, so his wala is hers. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who freed."
"15789","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: The woman inherits of wala."
"15790","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Qays the mawla of Amr informed me, from Ibn Tawus, from other than his father, from him, that he used to say: The woman inherits wala. And he recites: And for women is a share of what parents and near ones left."
"15791","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: If a woman frees a boy, and that boy has mawalis, she has their inheritance if they die, when their nearest mawla to them has died."
"15792","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that his mother, the daughter of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaa, had a mawla, and he had mawalis. The mawla died, then the mawalis of the mawla died, or some of them, and she secured their inheritances. He said: Umar informed me of that, from Jafar ibn al-Muttalib and others among them. He said: Abdullah ibn Kathir informed me the like of that, except he did not inform me from Jafar."
"15793","From al-Thawri, concerning two sisters, one of them bought her brother and freed him. Then the brother of this one who freed bought the father and freed him. Then the brother died. He said: His father inherits him, for he is most retaining of inheritance. Then the father died, and one of his two daughters is a freed woman to him, so they both have two-thirds together, then the remainder is for the one who freed, for she is an asaba."
"15794","From Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Tamim al-Dari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever accepts Islam at the hands of a man, he is his mawla."
"15795","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who takes a man as mawla and he accepts Islam at his hands. He said: He pays blood-money on his behalf and inherits him. Mamar informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"15796","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, and from Yunus, from al-Hasan, they said: His inheritance is for the Muslims. From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the like of the report of Mamar, with the addition: He may transfer his wala where he wishes so long as blood-money has not been paid on his behalf."
"15797","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he was asked about a slave who is freed, having children whose mother is free. He said: When the father is freed, he draws wala. From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Umar, the like of it."
"15798","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from al-Aswad, that Shurayh used to judge, when the father was a slave and the mother free, having children: He judged that the wala of what she bore from her husband is a slave to the mawla of the mother, and that it took effect that day and does not transfer. Until al-Aswad ibn Yazid narrated to him that Ibn Masud said: The father draws wala when he is freed. So Shurayh judged with it afterwards."
"15799","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that Shurayh used to judge that their wala belongs to the mawla of the mother, and that it took effect that day and does not transfer, and that Zayd ibn Thabit used to say it, until Masruq ibn al-Ajda informed him that Ibn Masud said: When their father is freed, he draws their wala. So Shurayh adopted it."
"15800","From Mamar, from Yazid al-Rishk, that Ali ibn Abi Talib judged their wala to their father, and that he drew wala when he was freed."
"15801","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Humayd al-Araj informed me that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi informed him that al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam came to Khaybar, and there were youths whose elegance and vigor pleased him. He said: Who are these? It was said to him: Mawalis of Rafi ibn Khadij. He said: And from where? They said: A slave of the bedouins married a freed woman of his, and she bore these. Al-Zubayr bought that slave, their father, for fifty dirhams and freed him. Then he took them out of the wealth of Rafi and put them in his wealth. Then he came to Medina, sent to Rafi ibn Khadij, informed him of the news, and that they are mawalis: If you have a dispute, come to Uthman. He came to Uthman and informed him of the news, and informed him of what al-Zubayr had done and said. He said: Uthman said: Al-Zubayr spoke the truth; they are his mawalis. He said: They are his mawalis to this day."
"15802","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Abd Allah ibn Urwa informed me, from al-Zubayr, that he came to a land of his in Khaybar, and there were youths in his land. He said: Who are these? It was said to him: Their mother is a freed woman of Rafi ibn Khadij, and their father is a slave. He bought their father and freed him. Then they disputed before Uthman, and he judged their wala for al-Zubayr. He said: Their sons are alive today."
"15803","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: Al-Zubayr passed by mawalis of Rafi and they pleased him. He said: Whose are these? They said: Mawalis of Rafi ibn Khadij. He said: And from where? It was said: Their mother is a freed woman of Rafi, and their father is a slave of so-and-so, a bedouin man. Al-Zubayr bought their father and freed him, then said to them: You are my mawalis. Al-Zubayr and Rafi disputed before Uthman, and he judged their wala for al-Zubayr. Hisham said: When Muawiya came, they disputed concerning them also, and Muawiya judged for us concerning them. He said: They are our mawalis to this day."
"15804","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from Urwa, and Humayd al-Araj, from Ibrahim al-Taymi: That Rafi ibn Khadij disputed al-Zubayr concerning a freed woman of Rafi whose husband was a slave. Al-Zubayr bought him and freed him. They disputed before Uthman, and he judged wala for al-Zubayr. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and from a man, from al-Hasan, that they said the like of the word of Uthman."
"15805","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Safar, from al-Shabi, he said: The grandfather draws wala. Saying: Wala: A man died leaving his father a slave and his grandfather free. He said: The grandfather draws wala."
"15806","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When the mukatab pays half, he draws the wala to himself."
"15807","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that Abdullah ibn al-Musayyab ibn Abi al-Saib and Muhammad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Azhar both informed him that Marwan ruled concerning a slave who marries a free woman and she bears him children while he is a slave, then he is freed, that her children belong to their father's people. We said to Abdullah: perhaps he ruled that ... as long as he lives? He said: No, rather he drew their wala when he was freed to his father's patrons. [source has 000 gap]"
"15808","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Abi Mulaykah said to us: Urwah ibn Iyad informed me that he was present with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz when a man came to him and said: A freedwoman of ours was married by a slave belonging to so-and-so, and she bore him children, then so-and-so bought him and freed him, and he claims the wala of our clients belongs to him. He said: He spoke the truth, their wala belongs to him. He said: By Allah, he bought him for only four hundred dirhams. He said: Even if he had bought him for a hundred dirhams, and if you wished you could have bought him and freed him yourself."
"15809","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Their wala belongs to their mother's people. And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: We used to hear that. Ata said to me: Even if their father is freed, but their father inherits from them."
"15810","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A woman who has grown sons, who pays blood-money on her behalf? He said: Her asabah. I said: And do her male children inherit from her? He said: Yes. I said: Then her freedwoman dies leaving male children, who pays blood-money on their behalf? He said: Her sons now hold their wala, they pay blood-money on their behalf and inherit from them."
"15811","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Their wala does not transfer to their mother's patrons. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, the same. Mamar said: And it reached me from Maymun ibn Mihran and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the same."
"15812","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Raja ibn Haywah informed me that while he was with Abd al-Malik at the end of his caliphate, two men disputed before him about clients whose mother was free and whose father was a slave, then their father was freed after that. Abd al-Malik wanted to rule their wala for their father's people, so Qabisah ibn Dhuayb said to him: Umar ibn al-Khattab already ruled it for their mother's people. Abd al-Malik said to him: Know what you say, O Qabisah, for there has been in that what you know, meaning the ruling of Marwan. Qabisah said: That is the truth, and I shall consider. A man said: I did not know what Qabisah argued back to Abd al-Malik, except that I witnessed Abd al-Malik rule between those two men that the wala belongs to their mother's people."
"15813","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: Ali and al-Zubayr disputed about a freedman of Safiyyah, so Umar ruled the blood-money liability upon Ali and the inheritance for al-Zubayr."
"15814","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, he said: When a woman dies leaving freedmen, the inheritance belongs to her son and the blood-money liability is upon them. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla used to rule by it."
"15815","From Sufyan, about a woman who died leaving her father, her son, and her freedmen: Mughirah reported from Ibrahim: For the father is a sixth of the wala, and the rest for the son."
"15816","From al-Hakam: The wala belongs to the son. And it reached me from Zayd ibn Thabit that he said: The wala belongs to the son. Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata the same, and it is what Sufyan prefers."
"15817","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Khuthaym reported to us from Said ibn Jubayr and Mujahid, they both said: The wala belongs to their mother's people always, except that the father draws the wala as long as he is alive."
"15818","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man dies leaving his grandfather and his brother, then a freedman of the dead man dies, is the freedman's property not shared between the grandfather and the brother? He said: Yes indeed."
"15819","And Ata said, about a man who died leaving his father and his sons, he said: The wala of the freedman belongs to his sons."
"15820","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who died leaving his grandfather and his brother, and a freedman of the dead man then died, he said: I consider it for the grandfather. Al-Zuhri said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to contend with his own view that he is a father, and on that basis he shared between him and the brother in inheritance. Mamar said: And I heard other than al-Zuhri say: It is between them in halves."
"15821","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about a man who permitted his slave-girl and she freed a slave, then other people bought her, he said: The wala belongs to the first ones who sold her."
"15822","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about a man and his son, the father was freed by one people and the son by other people, he said: They inherit from each other by kinship, and the blood-money liability is upon those who freed each."
"15823","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Jafar ibn Muhammad narrate from his father, he said: It was found in the scabbard of the sword of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: The most hostile of people against Allah are three: one who kills other than his killer, or strikes other than the one who struck him, or shelters an innovator, so Allah accepts from him neither devotional act nor obligatory act. And whoever takes as patrons other than his own patrons is a disbeliever in what Allah sent down upon His Messenger."
"15824","From Ibrahim, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyab say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever takes as patrons other than his own patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah accepts from him neither devotional act nor obligatory act."
"15825","From Mamar, from Matar al-Warraq, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Amr ibn Kharijah, he said: I was under the chin of the camel of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while it was chewing its cud and its saliva was flowing over my shoulders, and I heard him say while preaching at Mina: Allah has given every one entitled his right, and there is no bequest for an heir. The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Whoever claims for other than his father, or attaches himself to other than those Allah blessed him with, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people."
"15826","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, he said: One who heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, informed me, while the saliva of the camel of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was flowing over his thigh, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, addressed us upon his camel and said: Charity is not lawful for me nor for my household. And he took a hair from his camel's hump and said: By Allah, not even what equals this, nor what weighs this."
"15827","Allah curses whoever claims for other than his father or attaches himself to other than his own patrons. The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Allah has given every one entitled his right, so there is no bequest for an heir."
"15828","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: Allah has given every one entitled his right, so there is no bequest for an heir. The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone, and their reckoning is upon Allah. Whoever claims for other than his father or attaches himself to other than his own patrons, upon him is the continuous curse of Allah until the Day of Resurrection. No woman shall spend anything from her house except with her husband's permission. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even food? He said: That is the best of our wealth."
"15829","Then he said: The borrowed item is to be returned, the granted milch-animal is to be given back, the debt is to be paid, and the guarantor is liable."
"15830","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ali, that he said: Whoever takes the client of a people as patron without the permission of their patrons, upon them is the curse of Allah, the angels, and the people; Allah accepts from him neither devotional nor obligatory act. He said: And he says: The sarf and the adl are the voluntary and the obligatory."
"15831","Mamar reported from Asim ibn Sulayman, he said: Abu Uthman al-Nahdi narrated to us that he heard Sad ibn Abi Waqqas and Abu Bakrah say: They heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever claims for a father other than his father, knowing that he is other than his father, Allah forbids Paradise to him. Asim said: I said to Abu Uthman: Two men have testified before you, sufficient for you. He said: Yes, as for one of them, meaning Sad, he was the first to shoot an arrow in the path of Allah, and as for the other, meaning Abu Bakrah, he came down to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was besieging the people of Taif, with twenty-three of their slaves, I think he said, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, freed them."
"15832","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, that he heard Umar say: We used to recite: Do not turn away from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you, or: it is disbelief in you to turn away from your fathers."
"15833","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Abbas informed me, from a man of the Ansar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: Whoever claims for other than his father, upon him is the curse of Allah."
"15834","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, they said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever claims for other than his father, knowing that he is other than his father, Allah forbids Paradise to him. Asim said: I said to Abu Uthman: Two men have testified before you, sufficient for you."
"15835","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Sulayman, he said: Abu Uthman al-Nahdi narrated to me, he said: I heard Ibn Malik say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever claims for other than his father, knowing that he is other than his father, Paradise is forbidden to him."
"15836","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Abu Mamar al-Azdi, and he is Abdullah ibn Shikhkhir, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: He disbelieves in Allah Almighty who claims a lineage other than his own lineage and disowns a lineage, even if it is slight. From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Abu Mamar, from Abu Bakr, the same."
"15837","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, he said: Al-Hakam informed me, from Mujahid, he said: Muawiyah claimed that a man of Azd called Abdullah ibn Amr should be called: Whoever claims for other than his father shall never smell the fragrance of Paradise, though its fragrance is found from a journey of five hundred years, and it was said seventy years."
"15838","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Adi ibn Adi, from his father or from his uncle, that a slave called Kaysan named himself Qays, claimed for his patrons, and joined Kufah. So his father rode to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: O Commander of the Faithful, he was born on my bed, then he turned away from me and claimed for his patrons and my patron. Umar said: Is Zayd ibn Thabit ... Do you not know that we used to recite: Do not turn away from your fathers, for it is disbelief in you. Zayd said: Yes. Umar said: Perhaps Allah ... Go, separate your son to your camel, then go with him and strike your camel one lash and your son one lash until you reach your family. [source passage partly unclear]"
"15839","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: They used to write at the beginning of their wills: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is what so-and-so bequeaths: He testifies that there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and that the Hour is coming without doubt, and that Allah will resurrect those in the graves. And he enjoins those he leaves of his family to fear Allah, set right their relations, and obey Allah and His Messenger if they are believers. And he enjoins upon them what Ibrahim enjoined upon his sons, and Yaqub: Allah has chosen the religion for you, so do not die except as Muslims. And Abd al-Razzaq mentioned it from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas, the same."
"15840","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard my father mention the will of Rabi ibn Khuthaym: This is what Rabi ibn Khuthaym acknowledges against himself, and calls Allah to witness over it, and Allah suffices as witness, as requiter of His righteous servants, and as rewarder: That I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, as Prophet. So I enjoin upon myself and whoever obeys me to worship Him among the worshippers, praise Him among those who praise, and sincerely advise the community of Muslims."
"15841","Al-Thawri reported from Umarah ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zurah, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, which charity is greatest in reward? He said: That you give it while you are healthy, stingy, hoping for life and fearing poverty, and do not delay until when it reaches the throat you say: For so-and-so this, and for so-and-so that, though it already belonged to so-and-so."
"15842","Al-Thawri reported from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Sinan al-Aslami, from Ibn Masud, he said: Those two bitter ones: withholding in life, and squandering at death."
"15843","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Masruq, that he said: I do not like to see a man stingy, healthy, greedy in his life, generous at his death."
"15844","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd, from Murrah, about His saying: And gives wealth despite love of it, he said: Ibn Masud said: That you give it while you are healthy, stingy, hoping for life and fearing poverty."
"15845","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa say: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: A third of your wealth has been made for you as an addition to your deeds."
"15846","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: It is not right for a Muslim man that three nights pass except that his will is with him. Salim said: Ibn Umar said: Three nights never passed me except that my will was with me, meaning: he considers what is his and what is against him."
"15847","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, in what he narrates from Allah, Blessed and Almighty: O son of Adam, two traits I have given you, neither of which belonged to other than you: I made for you a portion of your wealth at your death by which I show you mercy, or he said: by which I purify you, and the prayer of My servants upon you after your death."
"15848","Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah reported from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, that he heard Tawus say: There is no Muslim who dies without bequeathing except that his family are duty-bound to bequeath on his behalf. Ibn Jurayj said: So I presented to Tawus what Ibrahim informed me about the will, and I said: Is it so? He said: Yes."
"15849","From Ismail, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, he said: The will is only a completion of what one omitted of charity."
"15850","From Ismail, from Dawud, also from al-Qasim ibn so-and-so, or so-and-so ibn al-Qasim, he said: Ibn Hari al-Qushayri said to me: Did your father bequeath? I said: No. He said: Then do not leave him until you bequeath on his behalf. He said to me: The will is a completion of what was omitted of zakat or charity."
"15851","From Ismail, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Awn say: The will is in the place of charity, so it is more beloved to me, when the legatee is rich, that he leave it."
"15852","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, he said: We mentioned that al-Zubayr and Talhah used to press wills upon men, so he said: And what was upon them not to do so? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died and did not bequeath, and Abu Bakr bequeathed. So if one bequeaths it is good, and if he does not bequeath there is no harm."
"15853","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Sad ibn Ubadah asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about a vow that was upon his mother, so he ordered it to be fulfilled."
"15854","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: Sad ibn Ubadah came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: My mother had a vow upon her, shall I fulfill it? He said: Yes. He said: Will that benefit her? He said: Yes."
"15855","Ibn Uyaynah narrated from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah mention that his mother died while an itikaf was due upon her. He said: So I hastened with my brothers to Ibn Abbas and asked him, and he said: Perform itikaf on her behalf, and fast."
"15856","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he said to Tawus: Charity for the dead? He said: Bravo, bravo, and he marvelled at that."
"15857","Ibn Jurayj reported: Yala informed me that he heard Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, say: Ibn Abbas informed us that the mother of Sad ibn Ubadah died while he was absent from her, so he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died while I was absent from her, will it benefit her if I give something in charity on her behalf? He said: Yes. He said: I call you to witness that the garden of al-Mikhraf is charity on her behalf."
"15858","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, informed him that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died and did not give anything in charity, will she have reward if I give charity on her behalf? He said: Yes. He said: She left a garden, so I call you to witness that I have given it in charity on her behalf."
"15859","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata being asked: Does the dead have reward in what the living gives in charity on his behalf? He said: That has indeed reached us."
"15860","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I free a slave on behalf of my mother though she has died? He said: Yes."
"15861","From Ibn Jurayj, Mamar, and al-Thawri, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died and did not bequeath, shall I bequeath on her behalf? He said: Yes. He said: And a man of Khatham came and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is a very old man who cannot perform Hajj except laid across his camel, shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes."
"15862","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ubaydullah ibn Umayr, from Abdullah ibn Abd, from Ibn Umayr, he said: The mother of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf died while he was absent from her and she did not bequeath. He said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died while I was absent and did not bequeath, and nothing prevented her from bequeathing except my absence. Tell me, if I give charity for her or free a slave for her, will she have reward? He said: Yes. He said: So he freed ten slaves on her behalf."
"15863","Mamar and al-Thawri narrated from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died suddenly, and I know that had she spoken she would have given charity. Shall I give charity on her behalf? He said: Yes."
"15864","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: If a man gave in charity on behalf of a dead person even a trotter, Allah would accept it from him."
"15865","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr died in a sleep-place of his, so Aishah freed on his behalf old slaves of his estate."
"15866","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Let no one pray on behalf of anyone, nor fast on behalf of anyone, but if you must do something, give charity on his behalf, or gift it."
"15867","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: It was mentioned to us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, freed a slave-girl on behalf of a woman who died without bequeathing, and gave charity on her behalf with goods."
"15868","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, that al-As ibn Wail had slaves to free due upon him, so his two sons, Amr and Hisham, asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Do we have reward in what we free on his behalf? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No."
"15869","Mamar narrated from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: I think it is from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Upon al-As ibn Wail were a hundred slaves to free, so he placed upon his son Hisham fifty slaves and upon his son Amr fifty slaves. Amr mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Nothing is freed on behalf of a disbeliever, but had he been a Muslim and you freed on his behalf, or gave charity, or performed Hajj, that would reach him."
"15870","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah: Abu Lahab freed a slave-girl of his called Thuwaybah, and she had suckled the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Then one of his family saw Abu Lahab in a dream and asked him what he found. He said: I found no rest after you except that I was given to drink from this of mine, and he pointed to the hollow under his thumb, for my freeing Thuwaybah."
"15871","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Ali visited a client of theirs at death, and he said: O Ali, shall I not bequeath? Ali said: No, Allah, Blessed and Almighty, only said: If he leaves good, and you do not have much wealth. He said: And he had seven hundred dirhams."
"15872","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib visited a man of Banu Hashim in illness, and he said: Shall I bequeath? Ali said: Allah, Blessed and Almighty, only said: If he leaves good, and you have left only little wealth, so leave it for your children. So he prevented him from bequeathing."
"15873","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Tawus reported to us from his father that he used to say: It is not allowed for one who has little wealth and many heirs to bequeath a third of his wealth. He said: And Ibn Abbas was asked about eight hundred dirhams, and he said: That is little. I said to Ibn Tawus: Did he then name anything? He said: It is not right; my father used to reconcile between them."
"15874","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrated to us that Aishah was asked about a man who died leaving four hundred dinars and several children, so Aishah said: There is in this no surplus beyond his children. Ibn Jurayj reported: Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me from his mother, from Aishah, the same as the hadith of al-Thawri, except that he said: Aishah blamed him and said: That is little, or something like that."
"15875","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If his heirs are few and his wealth is much, there is no harm in reaching a third in his will. But if his wealth is little and his heirs are many, he should not reach a third."
"15876","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the Farewell Pilgrimage, and I fell ill with an illness close to death. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, visited me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have much wealth, and none inherits from me except a daughter of mine. Shall I bequeath two thirds of my wealth? He said: No. I said: Half of my wealth? He said: No. I said: A third of my wealth? He said: A third, and a third is much. O Sad, for you to leave your heirs rich is better for you than to leave them poor begging people. O Sad, you will not spend any spending seeking thereby the face of Allah except that you increase in rank and elevation. And perhaps you will be left behind so that Allah benefits some people through you and harms others through you. O Allah, carry through for my Companions their migration, and do not turn them back on their heels. But the wretched one is Sad ibn Khawlah; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pitied him, for he had died in Makkah."
"15877","From al-Thawri, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Amir ibn Sad, from Sad, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to visit him while he disliked dying in the land he had migrated from. He said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I bequeath all my wealth? He said: No. He said: Half? He said: No. He said: A third? He said: A third, and a third is much. For you to leave your heirs rich in good is better for you than to leave them dependent begging people for what is in their hands. Whatever spending you spend is charity, even the morsel you lift to your wife's mouth."
"15878","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs informed me, he said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas fell ill in Makkah, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed the Farewell Pilgrimage, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, will you leave me in Makkah? He stayed with him a day, then came to him the next day and greeted him. He said: Shall I die in Makkah, O Prophet of Allah? He said: I hope you will not die in Makkah until Allah benefits some people through you and harms others through you. Sad prayed not to die in Makkah, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, answer the prayer of Sad. He said: That was when he said: O Prophet of Allah, I have no child except a daughter, and I am a man of much wealth. Shall I bequeath two thirds among my brothers, meaning the Emigrants? He said: No. He said: Half? He said: No. He said: A third? He said: A third, and a third is much."
"15879","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Sad ibn Abi Waqqas said: O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth, and I have no child except a daughter. Shall I bequeath two thirds? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That is much. He said: Half? He said: That is much. He said: A third? He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, fell silent, so the matter proceeded upon that."
"15880","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: For me to bequeath a fifth is more beloved to me than to bequeath a fourth, and to bequeath a fourth is more beloved to me than to bequeath a third. And whoever bequeaths a third has left nothing."
"15881","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Ibrahim said: For me to bequeath a fifth is more beloved to me than to bequeath a fourth, and to bequeath a fourth is more beloved to me than to bequeath a third. And whoever bequeaths a third has left nothing."
"15882","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Abu Bakr bequeathed a fifth, and said: I bequeath what Allah approved for Himself. Then he recited: And know that whatever you take of spoils, a fifth of it is for Allah. And Umar bequeathed a fourth."
"15883","From al-Thawri, from one who heard al-Hasan and Abu Qilabah say: Abu Bakr bequeathed a fifth."
"15884","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: A fifth was more beloved to them than a fourth, and a fourth was more beloved to them than a third."
"15885","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If a man's heirs are few, there is no harm in reaching a third in his will."
"15886","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: A third is the middle, neither undercutting nor excess."
"15887","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Ransom yourselves from your Lord, O people. No man owns anything ... Let me not know a man stingy with Allah's right upon him, until when death comes to him he starts scattering his wealth here and there. He said: Then Qatadah says: Woe to you, O son of Adam, you were stingy and withholding, until when death came to you, you started scattering and distributing your wealth. O son of Adam, fear Allah, fear Allah, and do not combine two evils in your wealth: evil in life and evil at death. Look to your kin who are needy and do not inherit, and bequeath to them from your wealth in a fitting way."
"15888","From Mamar, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: A third is an effort, and it is allowed."
"15889","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah al-Salmani, he said: When a man dies with no covenant to anyone upon him and no asabah to inherit him, he may bequeath all his wealth as he wishes."
"15890","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Abu Maysarah Amr ibn Shurahbil, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said to me: You are among the most likely quarters of Kufah that one of you should die leaving neither asabah nor kin, so what prevents him, when so, from placing his wealth among the poor and needy."
"15891","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A woman in the time of Abu Musa al-Ashari saw that she would die on such-and-such day, so she distributed all her wealth, then she died at that time. Her husband came to al-Ashari and informed him. He said: What kind of woman was your wife? He said: She was the most deserving of women to enter Paradise, except the martyr in the path of Allah. Abu Musa said: Do you order me to overturn her affair? So he allowed it."
"15892","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, that he said, about one who has no patron of manumission: He places his wealth where he wishes, and if he does not do so it is in the public treasury."
"15893","From Mamar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud said to a man: O company of the people of Yemen, among you dies a man who does not know that his origin is from the Arabs, nor knows from whom he is. Whoever is so, when death comes to him, he may bequeath all his wealth as he wishes."
"15894","From Mamar, from a man of the people of Jazirah called Ishaq ibn Rashid, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about one who gives all his wealth in charity: If he places his wealth in a right cause, none is more entitled to all his wealth than he. But if he gives some heirs to the exclusion of others, he has only a third."
"15895","From Mamar, from Matar al-Warraq, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Amr ibn Kharijah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: There is no bequest for an heir."
"15896","From al-Thawri, from a sick man who said to a woman: Marry this son of mine, and your dower of a thousand dirhams is upon me, though the dower of her likes is five hundred dirhams, then he died of that illness, he said: It is hers from his wealth, and the heirs take it from his son, for he is only his son's guarantor whether he marries him or did not order him. [ruling as stated]"
"15897","Ibn Jurayj reported: I heard Ata say: Every will may be revisited."
"15898","Mamar narrated from Qatadah that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The seal of a will is its end. Mamar said: And Qatadah used to say: He has choice in his will, in manumission and other than it, to change in it what he wishes. Mamar said: It reached me that he mentioned it from Amr ibn Shuayb, from al-Harith ibn Abdullah, from Umar. Mamar narrated from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the same as the saying of Qatadah."
"15899","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, he said: A man may go back on his mudabbar."
"15900","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Tawus, Ata, and Abu al-Shatha say: A man's last instruction is more deserving than his first. They say: A man may change of his will what he wishes, in manumission and other than it."
"15901","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata and Tawus, they said: A man may change of his will what he wishes, in manumission and other than it."
"15902","From Mamar, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that Nafi ibn Alqamah wrote to Abd al-Malik asking him about a man who bequeathed a will in which some were freed, then he retracted in his will as long as he was alive."
"15903","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah and others of the scholars of Kufah, they said: Every owner of a will may go back in it as long as he is alive, except manumission. From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, the same."
"15904","From al-Thawri, about a woman who left twenty-five dirhams and a sheep worth five dirhams, and she bequeathed the sheep to one man and a sixth of her wealth to another man: Some of us say: The sixth impinges upon the owner of the sheep, so he has half a sixth of the sheep. And some of us say: The owner of the sixth has a seventh of the sheep. This is the matter of the common people."
"15905","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man may change in his will what he wishes, even if it is manumission."
"15906","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who bequeaths a will then bequeaths another, he said: If he does not change anything of the first, both are allowed within a third of his wealth."
"15907","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said: If a man bequeaths his third, then bequeaths other bequests after that, they share together in the third."
"15908","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says: My slave is for so-and-so, then says: Half my slave is for so-and-so, some of us say: Three quarters and a quarter, and some of us say: A third and two thirds, and the most beloved to me is a third and two thirds. Ibn Abi Layla said it, and the common people."
"15909","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: If he changes anything of his will, he has retracted all of it. Mamar said: So I asked Ibn Shubrumah, and he said: Nothing is reduced from it except what he changed."
"15910","And I heard Mamar, when asked about a man who said: A third of my wealth is for so-and-so, and for so-and-so his maintenance until he dies, he said: Half the third is set aside for his maintenance."
"15911","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Hujayr, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The likeness of one who gives all his wealth then sits, is as though he inherited a kalalah."
"15912","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that when Allah turned to him he said: O Prophet of Allah, part of my repentance is that I speak only truth, and that I strip myself of all my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you. He said: Then I keep my share at Khaybar. From Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, similar."
"15913","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Abu Lubabah, when Allah turned to him, said: O Prophet of Allah, part of my repentance is that I abandon the abode of my people in which I committed the sin, I think he said: I shall neighbor you, and strip myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A third of that suffices you, O Abu Lubabah."
"15914","From Mamar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about a man who gives all his wealth in charity, he said: If he places his wealth in a right cause, none is more entitled to his wealth than he. But if he gives some heirs to the exclusion of others, he has only a third. He mentioned it from al-Zuhri."
"15915","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab claimed that Abu Lubabah had many sins."
"15916","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man, not foolish, gives all his wealth in a right of ... and so he said: Gardens are not forbidden, but a third. [source unclear at haqq al-hur / al-haraih]"
"15917","From Mamar, he said: When fighting comes, plague falls, and the sea is embarked upon, only a third is allowed. But if he lives and had freed, his freeing is allowed."
"15918","Mamar narrated from Ibn Tawus, from his father, raising it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The likeness of one who gives all his wealth then sits, is as though he inherited a kalalah."
"15919","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: Charity is from surplus wealth, and begin with those you maintain, and the upper hand is better than the lower hand. He said: I said: What is his saying, from surplus wealth? He said: Do not give what is yours and sit asking people."
"15920","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The best charity is what is from surplus wealth, and begin with those you maintain, and the upper hand is better than the lower hand. He said: I said to Ayyub: What is from surplus wealth? He said: From the surplus of your dependents. From Mamar, from Hammam, that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrate from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the same as the hadith of Ayyub."
"15921","From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, from Urwah ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The giving hand is better than the lower hand."
"15922","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Hakim ibn Hizam a gift on the day of Hunayn, and he deemed it little, so he increased him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, which of your two gifts is better? He said: The first. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O Hakim ibn Hizam, this wealth is green and sweet. Whoever takes it with generosity of soul and good eating is blessed in it, and whoever takes it with covetousness of soul and bad eating is not blessed in it, and he is like one who eats and is not sated. And the upper hand is better than the lower hand. He said: And from you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And from me. He said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall never take anything from anyone after you ever. He said: So he accepted neither stipend nor gift until he died. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: O Allah, I call You to witness over Hakim ibn Hizam that I call him to his right from this wealth while he refuses. He said: By Allah, I shall take nothing from you nor from any other."
"15923","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What prevents one of you from being like Abu so-and-so, who when he went out said: O Allah, I have given my honor in charity to Your servants, so if anyone reviles him he does not revile back."
"15924","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm: Amr ibn Sulaym al-Ghassani bequeathed, when he was ten or twelve years old, a well of his valued at thirty thousand, so Umar ibn al-Khattab allowed his will."
"15925","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that Amr ibn Sulaym al-Ghassani said: It reached Umar that a boy of Ghassan was dying, so he said: Order him to bequeath. So he bequeathed the well of Jusham, and it was sold for thirty thousand, while he was ten years old, or twelve, close to it."
"15926","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, he said: A boy of ours who had not reached puberty bequeathed to a paternal aunt of his in Syria much wealth valued at thirty thousand, so Abu Ishaq raised that to Umar ibn al-Khattab and he allowed his will."
"15927","Mamar narrated from Abu Ishaq, he said: I disputed before Shurayh about a boy who bequeathed to his foster-mother forty dirhams, and Shurayh allowed it."
"15928","Al-Thawri narrated from Abu Ishaq, he said: A boy of ours called Marthad, when he shed his teeth, bequeathed to his foster-mother of the people of al-Hirah, so Shurayh allowed his will, and said: When the minor hits the truth we allow it."
"15929","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: Whoever hits the truth, minor or major, we allow him, and whoever misses the truth, minor or major, we reject him."
"15930","Mamar and al-Thawri narrated from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Abdullah ibn Utbah was brought a case of a slave-girl who bequeathed, and they kept belittling her, so Abdullah ibn Utbah said: Whoever hits the truth we allow his will."
"15931","Mamar narrated from Simak ibn al-Fadl that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to say about a boy who had not reached puberty: I do not see that he should reach all a third of his wealth in his will. He said: And something near that is allowed for him."
"15932","Mamar narrated from al-Zuhri, he said: The will of a boy is allowed when he understands."
"15933","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you know ... when the minor boy and minor girl reach it their will is allowed? He said: I do not know it. [source has 000 gap]"
"15934","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me that Abd al-Malik ruled about a boy of Damascus who bequeathed, saying: When he reaches twelve years his will is allowed. He said: He kept acting by it and ruling by it until Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz came, and we feared he would overturn it, but Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz also ruled by it, and it remained upon it after."
"15935","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said: When the boy places the will in its right place it is allowed."
"15936","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The will of a boy is not allowed until he reaches puberty."
"15937","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: The fool is as he is, he said: And the confused, is their will allowed, even if they bequeath while overpowered in mind? He said: I do not consider they have a will. And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"15938","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: The will of a boy is not allowed until he reaches puberty."
"15939","Hushaym reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: No will, no gift, no present, no manumission is allowed until he reaches puberty, and the slave-girl until she menstruates."
"15940","And al-Thawri mentioned from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: The will of a boy is not allowed until he reaches puberty."
"15941","Hisham reported from al-Hasan, and al-Awzai from Wasil, from Mujahid, he said: The will of a boy is not allowed until he reaches puberty."
"15942","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Whoever bequeaths to a people naming them, leaving his needy kin, it is taken from them and returned to his kin. If there are no poor among his family, then to the poor folk, whoever they are. And if he bequeaths ... to whom he bequeathed it. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the same. [source has 000 gap at end]"
"15943","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When he bequeaths to the needy, his needy kin are begun with. But if he bequeaths to a people naming them, we give whom he named. From Mamar, and Qatadah said it from Ibn al-Musayyab, the same as the saying of al-Zuhri."
"15944","From Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ubaydullah ibn Yamur, a judge of Basrah, he said: Whoever bequeaths naming, we give whom he named. And if he says: He places it where Allah commanded, we give his kin."
"15945","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: Whoever bequeaths his third while having needy kin, they are given a third of the third."
"15946","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata, while I listened, about a man who bequeathed to a freedwoman of his, and he said: She is an heir. Ata said: She is not an heir; the heir is only whom Allah made an heir. But she is given from it the share of a woman. If that woman's share is more than a third, she returns to a third. And if the dead had bequeathed something in his third, she shares in it proportionally. He said: If a man bequeaths to a freedwoman a share of his inheritance, while the property is upon eight shares, she has the like of a man's share, a bequest like this other bequest."
"15947","Mamar reported from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: When he bequeaths outside his kin with a third, a third of the third is allowed for them, and two thirds of the third are returned to his kin."
"15948","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Whoever bequeaths naming, we give whom he named."
"15949","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A will, a man bequeaths in a matter, then I see better than it. He said: Then do what is better, as long as he did not name a person by name. But if he said: For the needy, and in the path of Allah, and you see better than that, then do what is better. Then he retracted from that and said: His word must be executed. He said: And his first saying is more pleasing to me."
"15950","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no bequest for a dead person."
"15951","From al-Thawri, he said: They say: If one bequeaths that the debt of so-and-so be paid off, though he had died, it is valid, because he bequeathed to the creditors."
"15952","From al-Thawri, he said: A killer has no bequest. Then he said: If the killer is killed, he has no bequest, and if he bequeaths that he be pardoned, a third is for the 'aqilah, and two-thirds are imposed."
"15953","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who bequeaths a bequest to a man, or gives him a gift, while he is absent, then the legatee or donee dies before the one who bequeathed to him, he said: Neither he nor his heirs get anything. Ma'mar said: I heard 'Uthman al-Batti say the like of that. From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, like the saying of al-Zuhri."
"15954","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who sends a gift with a man to another, then the giver perishes before it reaches the one to whom it was gifted, he said: It belongs to the heirs of the giver, unless he hands it to an executor or an agent."
"15955","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Fudayl, from Abu Hariz, from al-Sha'bi, that a man gifted a gift to a man, then died before it reached him, so he sent to 'Abidah al-Salmani, who said: If he had gifted it to the man before he died, the gift belongs to the heirs of the dead; and if he had gifted it to him after he died, the gift returns to the living, for the living does not gift to the dead."
"15956","From 'Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah, he said: I asked al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, he said: If he sends it with the messenger of the deceased, it belongs to the messenger of the deceased; and if it was with the messenger of the giver, it belongs to the giver."
"15957","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: A man bequeaths to a man, then the legatee dies, and the testator learns of his death but says nothing new about what he bequeathed to him [a word illegible in source], then the testator dies, he said: The bequest is for the family of the legatee. I said: Do they inform him? He said: No."
"15958","Ibn Jurayj reported: 'Ata said to me: Whatever a pregnant woman does with her pregnancy is a bequest. I said: Is it opinion? He said: Rather we heard it. 'Ata said: She and the nursing mother break fast in Ramadan if they fear for their children."
"15959","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whatever a pregnant woman does with her pregnancy is a bequest. Ma'mar said: One who heard 'Ikrimah told me he says the like of that."
"15960","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that he held whatever a pregnant woman does with her pregnancy is a bequest from the third. Al-Thawri said: We do not adopt that; we say: Whatever she does is valid, unless she is sick with an illness other than pregnancy, or her labor draws near."
"15961","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about the pregnant woman, he said: If she bequeaths, it is in the third."
"15962","Ma'mar reported, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that he said about a man who asks his heirs' permission at his death for a bequest and they permit him, he said: They have the choice when they shake their hands off his grave."
"15963","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: They have the choice when they retract."
"15964","From Ibn Jurayj, that 'Ata used to say: It is valid if they permit."
"15965","From Ma'mar, from 'Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: If they permit, it is binding upon them."
"15966","From Sufyan, he said: If the deceased bequeaths to an heir, and the heirs approve that in his life, they have the choice when he dies; if they wish they retract, because they approved what had not yet fallen to them and they did not own; they own only after death. If they approve after his death, it is valid, and they may not reject it, whether taken possession of or not."
"15967","I asked Hammad ibn Abi Hanifah, I said: How did your father rule about a man who bequeaths to some of his heirs? Saying: If the heirs approve, and otherwise it is for so-and-so, or for the poor. He said: He held it valid, saying: A man of the jurists said it, and Ma'mar narrated it, he said: It is valid as he said."
"15968","From Ma'mar, from Ash'ath ibn 'Abdullah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man does the deed of good for seventy years, then when he bequeaths he is unjust in his bequest, so he is sealed with his evil deed and enters Hell; and a man does the deed of evil for seventy years, then is just in his bequest, so he is sealed with his good deed and enters Paradise. He said: Then Abu Hurayrah says: Recite if you wish: These are the limits of Allah, up to: and for him a humiliating punishment."
"15969","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Harming in bequest is among the major sins. Then he said: These are the limits of Allah, and whoever transgresses the limits of Allah."
"15970","From al-Thawri, on His saying: Whoever alters it after hearing it, he said: It reached us that when a man bequeathed, his bequest was not changed, until: But whoever fears from a testator partiality or sin and reconciles between them, there is no sin upon him, was revealed, so he returns it to the truth."
"15971","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab bequeathed for the mothers of his children."
"15972","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that he bequeathed for his umm walad."
"15973","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: If a man gives his umm walad something and dies, it is hers. 'Abdullah told me, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the like of that."
"15974","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who bequeaths to the mothers of his children land to eat from as long as they do not marry; if they marry it returns to the heirs, he said: His bequest is valid on his condition."
"15975","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man bequeaths to his mother, while she is an umm walad of his father, or to the umm walad of his son, with a bequest, it is not valid, because she is owned by his son, and the inheritance returns to the heir."
"15976","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man bequeaths to his slave a third of his wealth, or a quarter of his wealth, the slave is freed from the third; and if he bequeaths to him specified dirhams, it is not valid."
"15977","I heard a man narrate from al-Hasan that he said: If he bequeaths to another's slave, it is valid."
"15978","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Jundab, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas: May a slave bequeath? He said: No, except with his masters' permission."
"15979","From al-Thawri, about one to whom something is bequeathed and the bequest perishes, he said: The legatee gets nothing; if all the wealth perishes except the bequest, the heirs share with him in that bequest."
"15980","From al-Thawri, about a man who frees a slave of his at his death, then says: Whatever remains of the third is for so-and-so, and it turns out the slave had been free before that, he said: The whole third belongs to the one he bequeathed to."
"15981","Al-Thawri said: If a man says: A third of my wealth is for the sons of so-and-so and the sons of so-and-so, the first being ten and the others seven, he said: Its third is between them in two halves. If he says: It is between so-and-so and the sons of so-and-so, it is by number."
"15982","From al-Thawri, about a man who says: A third of my wealth is for the sons of so-and-so, and they find him to be one, some said: He gets a third of the third; and some said: He gets half of the third. It is taken from Allah's saying: But if he has brothers, for his mother is the sixth."
"15983","From al-Thawri, about a man who bequeaths to the widows of the sons of so-and-so, al-Sha'bi said: It is for men and women; a man is called widower."
"15984","From al-Thawri, he said: If he bequeaths a third of his wealth saying: It is for so-and-so and so-and-so, then one of them dies, it belongs to the survivor; and if he says: It is between so-and-so and so-and-so, and one dies, the other gets half; and if he says: It is for so-and-so and for this minor, it all belongs to the man and the minor gets nothing; and if he bequeaths so-and-so's garment to so-and-so then buys it, it is nothing, because he bequeathed what he did not own."
"15985","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man bequeaths saying: For the sons of so-and-so, the daughters' children get nothing."
"15986","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says: My slave is for so-and-so, then says after: Half my slave is for so-and-so, some of us say: Three-quarters and a quarter; and some of us say: A third and two-thirds. Ibn Abi Layla and the generality said it."
"15987","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man bequeaths a bequest, then the legatee rejects it before the testator dies, his rejection is nothing; he may return to it if he wishes, because he rejected what had not yet fallen to him; and if he rejects after the testator's death, the rejection stands and he may not return to it; and if the legatee dies after the testator, and his heirs say: We do not accept it, that is not a rejection, because the bequest was not theirs; it was wealth they inherited."
"15988","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man bequeaths a brother of his, or a mahram relative, with emancipation, and he says: I do not accept, it is valid; he may not reject anything, because when he bequeathed to him the emancipation took effect; his rejection before or after the testator's death is nothing."
"15989","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, about a man who buys and sells while sick, he said: It is in the third, even if he lasts ten years."
"15990","From al-Thawri, he said: Every sick man who sells in his illness what is worth a hundred for fifty, the excess is a bequest; or buys what is worth fifty for a hundred, the excess is a bequest."
"15991","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says: Write a kitabah contract for my slave for a thousand dirhams, while his price is five hundred dirhams, and he bequeaths nothing else; or he says: Sell my house for a thousand dirhams while its price is a thousand, that is nothing, he bequeathed nothing; and if he says: Write kitabah for my slave or sell my house for a thousand dirhams, while its value is a thousand and a hundred, it is valid, because he made the hundred the bequest."
"15992","Al-Thawri reported, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah ibn Zufar, he said: A man of Hamdan came to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud on a piebald horse, saying: A man bequeathed to me an estate of his, and this is of his estate; shall I buy it? He said: No, do not buy anything of his wealth."
"15993","Al-Thawri reported, from Ash'ath, from Nafi', that he used to borrow from the orphan's wealth, entrust it, and give it as mudarabah."
"15994","From al-Thawri, from Mujahid, on His saying: Do not approach the orphan's wealth, he said: Do not lend from it. 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar narrated, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, the like."
"15995","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, both said: If a man bequeaths something obligatory upon him - hajj, expiation of oath, fasting, zihar, or the like - it is from the whole wealth."
"15996","Hisham ibn Hassan narrated, from al-Hasan, about a man who bequeaths what is obligatory upon him - hajj, zihar, oath, or the like - he said: It is from the whole wealth. He said: Ibn Sirin said: It is from the third."
"15997","From Ma'mar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: It is in the third. Al-Thawri said it from Ibrahim."
"15998","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The bequest is where its owner places it, unless the executor is suspect, then the ruler transfers it. He said: There is no harm in a man appointing a woman as executor if she is not suspect."
"15999","Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhri, he said: The bequest of the idiot, the delirious, and the deranged, and their charity and emancipation, are not valid, unless it is witnessed of him that he was sane."
"16000","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who bequeaths a third of his wealth to a man, then is killed by mistake, he said: The legatee also shares a third of the blood-money."
"16001","From 'Abdullah ibn Muharrar, he said: Al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah told me, he said: A man went out traveling, bequeathed a third of his wealth to a man, then was killed on that journey; his case was raised to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, who gave him a third of the wealth and a third of the blood-money."
"16002","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote about a man who bequeaths a slave to a man while having slaves, without naming him, writing: He is to be given the lowest of them, meaning the worst of them."
"16003","Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhri, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man ibn Bashir and Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf told me, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: My father Bashir ibn Sa'd took me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness a gift to me, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you gifted all your sons the like of this? He said: No. He said: Take it back."
"16004","From Ibn Jurayj, Ibn Shihab said: From Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: Bashir ibn Sa'd took me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have gifted this son of mine a slave; I came to you to witness you to it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you gifted all your children? He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, then."
"16005","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him narrate that Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man and Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrate, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: My father Bashir went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have gifted this son of mine a slave; I came to you to witness you to it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you gifted all your children? He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, then."
"16006","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Awn ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah told me, from al-Sha'bi, that al-Nu'man ibn Bashir - his mother said: O Bashir, gift al-Nu'man; they claim al-Nu'man's mother is the daughter of 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah - she kept at him till he gifted him. She said: Have the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) witness it. He went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned witnessing it to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you gifted your sons the like? He said: No. He said: I do not witness injustice. 'Awn said to me: As for me, I heard my father say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make them equal."
"16007","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Bashir ibn Sa'd brought his son al-Nu'man to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness a gift he gifted him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you gifted all your sons the like of this? He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be fair among your children, and refused to witness."
"16008","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Bashir ibn Sa'd, father of al-Nu'man, with his son al-Nu'man, who said: Bear witness that I have gifted him a slave or a slave-girl. He said: Have you other children? He said: Yes. He said: Have you gifted them what you gifted him? He said: No. He said: I witness only to the truth; I do not witness to this. I said: Did you hear it from your father? He said: No."
"16009","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: Is equalizing gifts among children a duty by Allah's Book? He said: Yes, it reached us from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Have you made your children equal? I said: About al-Nu'man ibn Bashir? I said: And about others?"
"16010","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah divided his wealth among his sons in his life; a son was born to him after he died; 'Umar met Abu Bakr, saying: I did not sleep tonight over this newborn son of Sa'd, who left him nothing. Abu Bakr said: Nor did I, by Allah, sleep tonight, or as he said, over him. So come with us to Qays ibn Sa'd; we will speak to him about his brother. They came and spoke to him. Qays said: As for what Sa'd executed, I will never revoke it, but I call you to witness my share is for him."
"16011","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata told me: Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah divided his wealth among his sons, then died while his wife was pregnant without his knowing of her pregnancy; she bore a boy. Abu Bakr and 'Umar sent about that to Qays ibn Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah. He said: As for a matter Sa'd divided and executed, I will never return to it, but my share is for him. I said: Did he divide by Allah's Book? He said: We find they divided only by Allah's Book."
"16012","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Amr ibn Dhakwan told me that Dhakwan Abu Salih told him this report, the report of Qays, that he divided his wealth among his sons, then went to Syria and died."
"16013","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I do not suspect told me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): An Ansari man invited him; a son of his came, and he kissed him, embraced him, and seated him beside him; then a daughter of his came, and he took her hand and seated her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you been just it would be better for you; be fair among your children even in kisses."
"16014","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to 'Ata: An heir gifts his sons - should he equalize among them, and between a father or a wife? Is he obliged to gift his father and his wife by Allah's Book with his children? He said: He mentioned only children. I heard nothing from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but that."
"16015","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, that he said: Do not prefer one over another by a hair. He used to say: Gifts are void; it is Satan's work. He used to say: Be just among them. I said: Some of their gift perished the day their father died. He said: The one gifted gets the like of it from his father's wealth. He said: And I say: No, the gift has ceased, and it is due when he is just among them."
"16016","From Zuhayr ibn Nafi', he said: We asked 'Ata ibn Abi Rabah, I said: I wanted to prefer some of my children in a gift I gift him? He said: No, and refused me strongly, saying: Make them equal."
"16017","Ibn Jurayj reported, from 'Ata, he said: A gift at death is in the third."
"16018","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, he disliked preferring some over others, while Abu al-Sha'tha' permitted it."
"16019","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, she said: When Abu Bakr's death approached, he said: O my daughter, none is more beloved to me in wealth than you, nor dearer to me in poverty than you; I had gifted you the harvest of twenty wasqs of my land at al-Ghabah; had you taken possession it would be yours; since you did not, it is for the heir; it is your two brothers and two sisters. 'A'ishah said: Is she but Umm 'Abdullah? He said: Yes, and the one in the belly of Ibnat Kharijah; it has been cast into my mind she is a girl; be good to her."
"16020","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah told me that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr told him that Abu Bakr said to 'A'ishah: O my daughter, I gifted you a gift from Khaybar; I fear I preferred you over my children; since you did not take possession, return it to my children. 'A'ishah said: O my father, had Khaybar with its harvest been mine I would return it."
"16021","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari told me they heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab say: What is with people who gift their sons, then when the son dies the father says: My wealth, in my hand; and when the father dies he says: I had gifted my son such-and-such. No gift except for who took possession and received from his father."
"16022","From Ma'mar, al-Zuhri said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib told me, he said: When it was 'Uthman's time that was complained to him, 'Uthman said: We looked into these gifts and saw the most entitled to take possession for the child is his father."
"16023","From Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Shurayh was asked: What is valid for a child of gifts? He said: When it is witnessed and announced. It was said: And his father takes possession for him? He said: He is the most entitled to take possession for his son."
"16024","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: Is nothing of gifts valid but what is handed to one who reached possession age, even if unmarried, if not foolish? He said: So they claim. He said: I was told from 'A'ishah that Abu Bakr gifted 'A'ishah a gift; when death approached he called her, saying: O little one, you are the dearest of people to me, and I wish you return to me what I gifted you. She said: Yes."
"16025","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa claimed that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to him: Any man who gifts one who reached possession age but does not hand it to him, that gift is void. They claim he took it from Abu Bakr's gift to 'A'ishah; he did not separate it for her, so he returned it when death came."
"16026","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote that nothing of gifts is valid but what is set aside, singled out, and announced."
"16027","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubrumah, about a man who gifts his son a third or quarter of his land, without dividing with him except by distinction, he said: He gets only what he takes from the people. Ma'mar said: One of our companions told me, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, that he held it valid, saying: Distinction is possession."
"16028","From Ma'mar, from 'Uthman al-Batti, about a man who gifts a son a known share of his in land, not having divided with his partners, he said: If he has exited all his right to him it is valid, when he takes possession with his partners, even if undivided."
"16029","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Ibn Shubrumah about it, who said: It is not valid till divided. Ma'mar said: 'Uthman al-Batti's saying is dearer to me, saying: They only want to enrich the divider."
"16030","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubrumah: He did not regard possession by some heirs as anything. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri used to permit it."
"16031","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever gives a gift hoping its reward, it returns to its owner, or he is rewarded for it; and whoever gives in a right or kinship, we validate his gift. From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from 'Umar the like."
"16032","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: A man gifted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he rewarded him, but he was not pleased, so he increased him, but he was not pleased, so he increased him, I think he said three times. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I intended not to accept a gift. Sometimes Ma'mar said: Not to accept gifts except from a Qurashi, Ansari, Thaqafi. From Ma'mar and Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, the like, adding: or Dawsi."
"16033","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, he said: Whoever gives for ties, kinship, right, or kindness, we validate his gift; and the profiteering side, its gift is returned to him, or he is rewarded from it."
"16034","From Yazid ibn Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote that the Muslim marries the Christian woman, while the Christian does not marry the Muslim woman; the emigrant marries the Bedouin woman, while the Bedouin does not marry the emigrant woman, to take her out of her land of emigration; and whoever gifts a gift to a relative, his gift is valid; and whoever gives to a relative and is not rewarded for his gift, he is most entitled to it."
"16035","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever gifts a gift to a relative may not return to it; and whoever gifts a gift to a non-relative may return to it, unless rewarded."
"16036","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Abza, from 'Ali, he said: Whoever gifts a gift to a relative and is not rewarded for it, he is most entitled to his gift."
"16037","From al-Aslami, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr told me, from his father, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever gives something unasked has no reward for his gift; and if asked and he gives, he is most entitled to his gift till rewarded for it till he is pleased."
"16038","He said: From al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever gifts a gift to a non-relative and he takes possession, he is most entitled to return to it as long as not rewarded for it, or consumed, or one of them dies."
"16039","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: A gift is not valid till taken possession; charity is valid before taken possession."
"16040","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about a man partner with his son in wealth, whose father says: You have a hundred dinars of the wealth between me and you, he said: Abu Bakr and 'Umar ruled it invalid till he takes possession of it from the wealth and sets it aside."
"16041","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Ibn Shubrumah about it, who said: If he names and assigns him a hundred dinars of his wealth it is valid; and if he names a third or quarter it is invalid till he divides it."
"16042","From Ma'mar, from 'Uthman, about a man who gifts another a gift and he takes possession, then the giver returns to it; the donee says: I have returned it to you; then the giver dies before taking possession from the donee, he said: It is nothing; it belongs to the donee till he takes possession as it was taken from him."
"16043","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata: The giver - what he gives to a relative seeking no reward has no reward; and whoever gives [a word illegible in source] seeking reward is most entitled to what he gave till rewarded. I said: Do you say so? He said: Yes."
"16044","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, that he said: Whoever gifts a gift without stipulating a condition it is valid. Mu'adh of Yemen ruled: Any man who gifts land on condition you hear and obey me, and he hears and obeys, it belongs to the donee; and any man who gifts such-and-such till a term, then it returns to him, it belongs to the giver when the term comes; and any man who gifts land without stipulating, it belongs to the donee; so it is with conditions. Mu'adh ruled so among them in Islam."
"16045","From al-Thawri, he said: We say: The relative is the relative. He said: We say reward is not till he gifts him and says: This is the reward of what you gave me, even if he gives him the like of that."
"16046","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have not the evil example; the one returning in his gift is like the dog returning to its vomit."
"16047","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one returning in his gift is like the dog returning to its vomit; we have not the evil example."
"16048","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one returning in his gift is like the dog returning to its vomit. Ma'mar said: One who heard al-Hasan told me he narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Al-Hasan used to say: He does not return in gifts."
"16049","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: How does a man return in his gift?"
"16050","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me, from Tawus, he said: I used to hear as a boy the boys saying: The one returning in his gift is like the dog returning to its vomit, not realizing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coined that as an example, till I was later told that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of one who gifts then returns in his gift is like the dog that vomits then eats its vomit."
"16051","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me, from Tawus, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for anyone to gift something then take it back except the father."
"16052","From Isma'il, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The one returning in his gift is like the dog returning to its vomit, except the father from his child."
"16053","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Musa say to 'Ata while I listened: A man gifted a foal; it grew with him, then the giver returned to it. He said: I see it appraised at its value the day he gifted it. Sulayman ibn Musa said: A man in Syria did that; 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote: Only women and the worst of men return in gifts. Stop the giver publicly; if he returns to it, value it at its value the day he gifted it, or the like of the foal the day he gifted it; let him pay it to the giver."
"16054","From Ma'mar, from a man of the Jazirah, that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote about a man who gifted a gift to a man, then its owner took it back, writing it be returned to him publicly as he gifted it publicly."
"16055","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote: Whoever gifts a gift to a non-relative may not return to it; he has the like of his gift the day he gifted it if it grew. Sufyan said: Meaning: He returns to it only publicly before the ruler. Ibn Abi Layla used to say: He returns to it without the judge."
"16056","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, asked about a man who gifted his son a she-camel, then returned to it; it was raised to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, who returned it to him as-is and assigned its increase to his son."
"16057","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub and 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote about a man who gifted a gift to a man and it perished, writing he return the value of his gift the day he gifted it."
"16058","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man returns in his gift; if consumed, he has the value of his gift the day he gifted it."
"16059","From al-Thawri, from Abu Bakr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and from Tawus, from al-Sha'bi, both said about gifts: If consumed, no return to it."
"16060","From Sufyan, he said: The meaning of consuming a gift is he sells it, gifts it, eats it, or it leaves his hand to another; that is consumption. Sufyan said: One pointed to used to say: If it changes or an event occurs in it, no return to it - e.g., land gifted to him and he sows crops in it, a garment he dyes, a house he builds, a slave-girl who bears, a beast that bears; if its giver returns to it while with the donee, he does not return to its children, for they were born with the donee and were not in what he gifted."
"16061","From Sufyan, he said: If a man gifts dirhams to a man, then the giver says to the donee: Lend them to me, and he lends them to him, they become a debt of the donee upon the giver; that is like consumption; no return to it. From Sufyan, he said: The giver does not return in his gift if the donee is absent."
"16062","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said to 'Ata while I listened: Does a woman return in what she gives her husband, her dower or other? He said: No."
"16063","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If she gifts him, or he gifts her, each one's gift is valid - meaning the spouses giving each other. From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, he said the like of Ibrahim's saying."
"16064","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When a woman came to Shurayh who had gifted her husband a gift then returned to it, he would say: Your proof that you gifted it to him willingly, without coercion or humiliation; otherwise, her oath by Allah that she gifted it only after dislike and humiliation."
"16065","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: He used to say about a woman giving her husband and a husband giving his wife, he said: I release her, but not him."
"16066","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: I saw the judges only releasing the woman in what she gifted her husband, not releasing the husband in what he gifted his wife."
"16067","Al-Thawri reported, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Abu al-Duha, from Shurayh, that a woman came disputing her husband over charity she gave him from her dower. Shurayh said: Had she been willing she would not come seeking it; so he did not validate it."
"16068","From Sufyan al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Ja'far, he said: I saw Shurayh when a woman came disputing her husband; he claimed she released him. Shurayh said to the evidence: Did you see the paper? They said: No. So he did not validate it."
"16069","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Thaqafi, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote that women give out of desire and fear; any woman who gives her husband and wishes to return may return."
"16070","Al-Thawri reported, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that he used to say: A woman returns in what she gave her husband while both alive; if they die, no return for them."
"16071","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: [text truncated in source: only] The man, his wife and his slave."
"16072","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from a man, from Mujahid, on His saying: If they willingly give you part of it, he said: Till death. From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, the like."
"16073","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Shubrumah, about a woman who gifts her husband then returns, he said: She is made to swear she did not gift him willingly, then her wealth is returned to her. He said: As for a woman who leaves her husband something before he consummates with her, it is valid. Ma'mar said: I know none who disagreed about it."
"16074","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, he said: There is no possession between a man and his wife; if she gifts him, or he gifts her."
"16075","Ma'mar reported, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no possession between them."
"16076","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: If each does not take possession of what his companion gifted him, it is nothing."
"16077","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: I gathered, I and Hammad and Ibn Shubrumah, with Ibn Nawf, amir of Kufah, about a woman whose husband gave her something. Ibn Abi Layla said: I and Hammad said: Her taking possession is his informing; she is in his household. Ibn Shubrumah said: She gets nothing till she takes possession. Sufyan said: Ibn Shubrumah's saying is dearer to me."
"16078","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar: 'Umar mounted a man on a horse in Allah's path, then saw it sold; 'Umar wanted to buy it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do not return in your charity."
"16079","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab gave charity of a horse, or mounted upon it, then found some of its offspring sold; he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I buy it? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it, it meets it and its offspring."
"16080","From Ma'mar, from 'Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Charity is for its day, and the sa'ibah is for its day - meaning the Day of Resurrection. Ma'mar said: Meaning: There is in it no return nor reward."
"16081","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Charity and the sa'ibah are for its day - meaning the Day of Resurrection."
"16082","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man returns in his gift if he gifted it seeking reward; he does not return in his charity."
"16083","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Ibn 'Umar would free no Jew nor Christian, except he once gave charity upon his son of a Christian slave; that son died, so Ibn 'Umar inherited that Christian slave and freed him because he had given charity of him. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: We know no harm in it, and we know none who disliked it except Ibn 'Umar."
"16084","Ma'mar said, from 'Asim, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Whatever a katib returns to you is lawful."
"16085","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whatever Allah's Book returns to you is lawful."
"16086","From Ma'mar, from a man of Abu Ishaq's companions, he said: He told me he asked al-Sha'bi about a servant he gave charity of to his mother. He said: It was said to me: It is not lawful for you to use her service. I asked al-Sha'bi, who said: Yes, use her service; when your mother dies she is yours as inheritance."
"16087","From al-Thawri, from 'Asim and Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, he said: Whatever Allah's Book returns to you, eat."
"16088","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal: A man gave charity to his mother of a boy; his mother wrote kitabah with him; he paid part of his kitabah, then his mother died; he asked 'Imran ibn al-Husayn, who said: He is yours, and you are most entitled to him; if you wish, carry it out for Allah's sake as you had assigned it."
"16089","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: 'Imran ibn al-Husayn was asked, while I listened - or he said: I asked 'Imran ibn al-Husayn - about a man who gave charity to his mother of a boy and ate of his yield, he said: He has no reward for what he ate of it, or the like of this."
"16090","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata said to me about charity: I dislike it be inherited unless the heir assigns it to that cause. Then 'Ata mentioned to me the affair of 'Alqamah; I wrote it in al-Wala'."
"16091","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I prefer he not eat the charity he gave, but take other than it of the wealth."
"16092","From 'Abdullah ibn 'Ata, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, I gave charity to my mother of a slave-girl, then my mother died. He said: You have your reward, and the inheritance returned her to you."
"16093","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm: An Ansari man gave charity of a wall of his; his father came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning their need of it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave it to his father; then the father died and his son inherited it."
"16094","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar and 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr and Humayd al-A'raj, all from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, that 'Abdullah ibn Zayd al-Ansari gave charity of a wall of his; his father came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning their need, or the like. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it to his father; then his father died, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it [text ends abruptly in source]."
"16095","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri and Hammad and Ibn Shubrumah, they said: Charity is not valid till taken possession."
"16096","From Hushaim ibn Bashir, from al-Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi: Shurayh and Masruq would not validate a charitable gift until it was taken into possession."
"16097","From Hushaim ibn Bashir, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: A charity gift is not valid except a charity that has been taken into possession."
"16098","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, he said: Yahya ibn Ja'dah narrated to me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The joker and the serious are alike in charity. From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abd Allah ibn Nujayy, from Ali the same."
"16099","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman: Ali and Ibn Mas'ud would validate charity even if it was not taken into possession. He said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Shurayh would not validate it until taken into possession, and the view of Mu'adh and Shurayh is more beloved to Sufyan."
"16100","Al-Thawri reported, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When charity is announced it is valid even if not taken into possession; meaning a slave, or a slave-girl, or a house, and the like."
"16101","From Sufyan, he said: If a man says to a man, give my wealth in charity to whomever you wish, he may not take it for himself, but let him give it to a relative, or a child if he wishes."
"16102","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, about a man who gave charity to people while sick and they did not take possession until the donor died, he said: It is from the third. From Ma'mar, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: It is nothing."
"16103","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: No gift of a woman in her wealth is valid until she gives birth, or reaches maturity, and that is the Sunnah, and until she loves wealth and keeping it, and loves profit and hates loss."
"16104","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: No gift of a woman in her wealth is valid until she gives birth, or reaches maturity, and that is the Sunnah. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah the same."
"16105","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: It reached me that no new disposal of a woman in her wealth is valid until she gives birth, or a year passes over her in her house after consummation. I said: Neither gift, nor manumission, nor anything in the path of Allah except by the father's view? He said: Yes. I said to Ata: Is it established? He said: Yes, they claimed."
"16106","Ibn Jurayj reported, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu al-Sha'tha said: No gift of a young unmarried girl is valid until she bears her like. I said to Amr: What about manumission? He said: All that is the same."
"16107","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: If she grows old and remains unmarried, meaning by unwedded old age, while she is a grown unmarried girl never yet married in her house, how? He said: It is valid for her, that is only about the young girl; when she grows old and understands, it is valid for her."
"16108","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When a young married woman gives a gift before a year, and does not revoke until she dies, it is valid. Ayyub said: I did not see people follow him on that."
"16109","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing of a woman in her wealth is valid except with her husband's permission, when he holds her marriage bond."
"16110","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that a married woman has no bequest in her wealth at all except with her husband's permission."
"16111","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz allowed the woman, when she and her husband dispute about her wealth, and she says: I want to uphold what Allah commanded, while he says: You harm me, he allowed her a third during her life."
"16112","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a woman gives from her wealth without foolishness or harm, her gift is valid even if her husband dislikes it."
"16113","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Simak, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about a woman who gave from her wealth: If she is not foolish nor intending harm, validate her gift."
"16114","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Aishah, she said: Hind came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, there was no household on earth I loved Allah to humiliate more than your household, and there is no household on earth I love Allah to honor more than your household. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And also, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul. Ma'mar said: Meaning you will increase."
"16115","Then she said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a withholding man, so is there sin on me if I spend on his dependents from his wealth without his permission? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no harm on you to spend on them with fairness."
"16116","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, he narrated it from Aishah: Hind, mother of Mu'awiyah, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man, and he does not give me except what I take from him without his knowledge. She said: Is there anything on me in that? He said: Take what suffices you and your sons with fairness."
"16117","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah: Asma daughter of Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, I have nothing except what al-Zubayr brings to me, shall I spend from it? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spend, and do not withhold lest it be withheld from you."
"16118","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman from her husband's wealth except fresh produce. Qatadah said: Meaning what is not stored: bread, meat, and condiment."
"16119","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, my wife gives from my wealth without my permission. He said: You two are partners in reward. He said: But I forbid her. He said: For you is what you withheld, and for her is what she did well."
"16120","From Isra'il, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to me, from Ikrimah freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a woman came to him and said: Is it lawful for me to take from my husband's dirhams? He said: Is it lawful for him to take from your jewelry? She said: No. He said: Then his right over you is greater."
"16121","From Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah: He was asked about a woman giving charity from her husband's wealth, he said: No, except from her sustenance, and the reward is between her and her husband, and it is not lawful for her to give anything in charity from her husband's wealth except with his permission."
"16122","Al-Thawri reported, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman spends from her husband's food without spoiling, she has her reward, and her husband has the like, and neither diminishes the other at all, and the storekeeper has the like: for her for what she spent, and for him for what he earned."
"16123","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from a woman: She was with Aishah when a woman asked her: May a woman give charity from her husband's house? She said: Yes, so long as she does not guard her wealth with his wealth."
"16124","From Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No woman shall spend anything from her husband's house except with her husband's permission. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even food? He said: That is the best of our wealth."
"16125","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: A man may take back from his child what he gave him of his wealth, so long as it has not died or been consumed, or a debt has not attached to it. Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote the same."
"16126","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A man may take back from his child what he gave him of his wealth, but the child may not take back from the father what he gave him of his wealth, due to his right over him."
"16127","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: A man may take from his son's wealth what he wishes, and if she is a slave-girl he may take her as concubine if he wishes. Qatadah said: What he said about the slave-girl does not please me."
"16128","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man may not take anything from his child's wealth except when needy, spending with fairness; his son supports him as the father used to support him. But if the father is wealthy, he may not take his son's wealth to guard his own wealth thereby, or to put it in what is not lawful."
"16129","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or Abu Bakr, or Umar said to a man who blamed his son for something he withheld: Your son is an arrow from your quiver."
"16130","From al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have wealth, and I have dependents, and my father has wealth and dependents, and my father wants to take my wealth. He said: You and your wealth belong to your father."
"16131","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: But his father is independent of him? He said: His father should not harm him while his son dislikes it. He said: I said to Ata: His father wanted to increase in wives, food, and living. He said: His father has more right to it so long as he does not take it elsewhere. I reviewed it with him, and he said: So, and I repeated it to him, and he said: His father has more right to it."
"16132","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata said to me: It was said: His wealth did not avail him, and what he earned; his child is his earning. And Mujahid and Aishah said it."
"16133","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ibn Abbas, he said: His child is his earning."
"16134","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: A man may take from his son's wealth with fairness."
"16135","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I heard Ata say: A man may hire his son for work when his father is in need."
"16136","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man of knowledge narrated to Ata that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The wealth of the child, its wholesome is the most wholesome of wholesome."
"16137","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Husayn say: A man disputed with his father, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You and your wealth belong to him. Then he ordered about him. I said to him: Then he said: Take him away, and if he overcomes you then inform me of that, I will help you against him. He said: Then a man who disputed with his father went to Ali, like this story."
"16138","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, my father asks me for my wealth. He said: Give it to him. He said: He wants me to give it all out to him. He said: Give it out to him. He said: And a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he advised him: Do not disobey your parents, for if they ask you to strip yourself of your world for them, then strip yourself of it for them."
"16139","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah: A man, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advised him, the Prophet said to him: Be dutiful to your parents, and if they command you to strip yourself of all your wealth, then do so."
"16140","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: Is the Sunnah of the grandfather in what he takes from his grandson's wealth like the Sunnah of the father in what he takes from his son's wealth unwillingly? He said: If needed, yes, its owner takes at all. I said: Even if indebted? He said: Yes, but without need it is not like the father."
"16141","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May the grandfather not take from his grandson's wealth unwillingly while rich without need, even if he does not take it elsewhere? He said: No, and he is not like the father."
"16142","From al-Thawri, he said: A man is compelled to spend on his grandfather, his father's father."
"16143","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: When the orphan's mother is needy, spend on her from his wealth; her hand is with his hand. It was said: And the wealthy? He said: She has nothing."
"16144","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The orphan whose mother is needy, shall spend on her from his wealth? Ata said: Has she nothing? I said: No. He said: Yes, none is more entitled to consume his wealth than her. He said: I said to Ata: If she was a slave-girl not freed, shall she be freed with it? He said: Yes, he is compelled to free her if they do not enjoy her and need him."
"16145","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from an aunt of his: She asked Aishah about an orphan in her care, may she take from his wealth? Aishah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most wholesome what a man eats is from his earning, and his child is from his earning."
"16146","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from an uncle of his: He asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr: He inherited from his wife a slave-girl, he and his child, and wanted to approach the slave-girl. Sa'id said: Write its price on you as a debt to your child, then approach her."
"16147","From Bakkar, he heard Wahb say to a man like the word of Sa'id ibn Jubayr: Write its price for your child, then approach her."
"16148","Al-Thawri reported, from Amr ibn Qays, from a grandmother of his, she said: I disputed to Shurayh about a slave-girl of mine which my father gave as dowry to his wife, and I disputed him to Shurayh, and he judged the slave-girl for me, and judged that I pay its value to his wife."
"16149","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man take anything from his son's wealth without his son's order? His son is needy, and his father uses him? He said: No, and let his father fear Allah Almighty in him."
"16150","Ibn Jurayj reported, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu al-Sha'tha saw no harm in a man eating from his son's wealth what he ever eats, apart from his son, when his father has wearied him and not spent on him."
"16151","From al-Thawri, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: Every heir is compelled over his heir in maintenance if he has no means."
"16152","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: A man is compelled to maintain his parents even if polytheists, and to maintain his grandfather, his father's father, and to maintain his children so long as small; when they reach puberty he is not compelled to maintain them. He said: The mother is not compelled to maintain her children, small or grown, even if wealthy."
"16153","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The mudabbar is from the third."
"16154","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abjar, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh: He would put the mudabbar from the third, and Masruq would bring it out free apart from the third."
"16155","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi: Ali put the mudabbar from the third."
"16156","From al-Zuhri, Qatadah, and Hammad, they said: The mudabbar is in the third."
"16157","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, they said: The mudabbar is a bequest."
"16158","From al-Thawri, about a slave between two men, one grants him tadbir and the other withholds, he said: Hastening the value is more beloved to us."
"16159","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah: A man freed a boy of his after his death, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put him from the third."
"16160","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah: A man of the Ansar granted tadbir to a boy of his, leaving no other, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed a third of him."
"16161","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man grant tadbir to his slave while he has no wealth besides him? He said: No. Then he mentioned what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the slave granted tadbir in his time. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is more independent of him than such-and-such. Then Ata recited: And those who when they spend neither waste nor stint. And he mentioned what he said about a man who gives all his wealth in charity and sits with no wealth."
"16162","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold a mudabbar whose master needed its price. From Ma'mar, from Ibn al-Munkadir the same."
"16163","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar: He heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: A man in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed a slave of his after death, having no wealth besides him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy him from me? Nu'aym ibn Abd Allah al-Adawi said: I will buy him, and bought him. Amr said: Jabir said: A Coptic boy who died last year. And Abu al-Zubayr added in it: Called Ya'qub."
"16164","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: A man of the Ansar granted tadbir to a boy of his, having no wealth besides him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy him from me? A man of Banu Adi ibn Ka'b ibn al-Nahham bought him. Amr said: Jabir said: A Coptic boy, who died last year in the rule of Ibn al-Zubayr."
"16165","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Abu Madhkur freed a boy of his called Ya'qub the Copt after his death, and it reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Has he wealth besides him? They said: No. He said: Who will buy him from me? Nu'aym ibn al-Nahham, son-in-law of Umar ibn al-Khattab, bought him for eight hundred. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spend on yourself, and if surplus then on your family, and if surplus then on your relatives, and if surplus then distribute here and there."
"16166","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man grant tadbir to his slave having no wealth besides him? He said: No. Then he mentioned what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the slave granted tadbir in his time. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is more independent of him than such-and-such. Then Ata recited: And those who when they spend neither waste nor stint. And he mentioned what he said about a man who gives his wealth in charity and sits with no wealth."
"16167","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: Ibn al-Munkadir asked me about the mudabbar, he said: How did your father say about him? Did its owner sell him? He said: Yes. He said: I said: If in need? Ibn al-Munkadir said: Even if not in need."
"16168","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from one who narrated to him, from Amrah, she said: Aishah fell ill and her illness lengthened. She said: Her nephews went to a man and mentioned her illness, and he said: You tell me news of a bewitched woman. She said: They went to look, and there was a slave-girl of hers who had bewitched her, and she had granted her tadbir. She called her and asked her: What did you intend? She said: I wanted you to die so I am freed. She said: Then upon me for Allah is that you be sold to the harshest Arabs in ownership. So she sold her, and ordered its price be put in one like her."
"16169","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sold a mudabbar whose master's debt encompassed his neck."
"16170","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When his master has debt, he works for his price."
"16171","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, he said: A man may return about his mudabbar."
"16172","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Tawus saw no harm in a man returning about his manumission. Amr said: And he ordered me to write tadbir for a concubine of his. I said to him: Do you stipulate except that you see your view? He said: Why? I knew he says: Is it not right for me to return in it if I wish? I said to him: The judges do not judge by that today. So he ordered me to write for him what I said to him."
"16173","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, they said: The mudabbar is a bequest."
"16174","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The mudabbar is a bequest; its owner returns in it whenever he wishes."
"16175","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Selling the mudabbar is disliked. And I heard him say: Return is made in the mudabbar and in every bequest."
"16176","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi: They disliked selling the mudabbar."
"16177","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The bold sells him, and the Godfearing refrains from him."
"16178","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The mudabbar is not sold. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me he says the same."
"16179","From Ma'mar, he said: A man came to him and said: I was sent to you from Kufa to ask you about a man who granted tadbir to a slave-girl of his, then sold her, and the buyer had intercourse with her. He said: The slave-girl is returned, and the one who had intercourse pays the dowry, and she is left as she was."
"16180","From Ibn Uyaynah, or from another, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: No return is made in the mudabbar. From Ibn Uyaynah, and Ibn Abi Yahya."
"16181","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who bought a mudabbarah slave-girl then freed her, he said: His freeing is valid, and this seller buys with its price a slave-girl and grants her tadbir."
"16182","From Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrate: A man of the Ansar called Abu Madhkur freed a boy of his after his death, having no wealth besides him, called Ya'qub. It reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Who will buy him from me? Al-Nu'aym ibn Abd Allah al-Adawi bought him for eight hundred dirhams. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed its price to him and said: When one of you is poor, let him begin with himself; if surplus then with his dependents; if surplus then with his relatives; if surplus then here and there, and he pointed right and left."
"16183","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: The children of the mudabbarah are in her status."
"16184","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Qusayt, from Ibn Umar, he said: The child of the mudabbarah is in her status."
"16185","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The child of the mudabbarah is in their mother's status; if she bears them after tadbir, they are in her status."
"16186","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The child of the mudabbarah is in her status."
"16187","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The children of the mudabbarah are in their mother's status."
"16188","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about the mudabbarah who dies leaving a child she bore after tadbir, he said: In their mother's status. Ma'mar said: And one who heard Ikrimah informed me he says: No freeing upon them."
"16189","Ibn Jurayj reported, Ata informed me that Abu al-Sha'tha used to say about the mudabbar: His child are slaves, like a wall you give in charity when you die, and its fruit is yours while you live. From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Abu al-Sha'tha, the same."
"16190","Ibn Jurayj reported, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu al-Sha'tha used to say: The children of the mudabbar are slaves, and if she was pregnant the day of tadbir, her child is like the mudabbar, as if a limb of her."
"16191","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, he said: I attended Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan when dispute about the children of the mudabbarah was brought to him, and he consulted those around him. A man said to him: Her children are sold, for a man gives palms in charity and eats their fruit. The other said, opposing what his companion said: The mudabbarah, her child is in her status. He said: I thought he said: A man may gift a sacrificial camel and it bears, and its calf is slaughtered with it. Ikrimah said: He stood without judging anything about them."
"16192","Ma'mar reported, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote that the children of the mudabbarah be sold."
"16193","From Isma'il ibn Abd Allah, he said: Ibn Awn informed me, he said: I was with al-Qasim ibn Muhammad when a Bedouin asked him: A man freed a slave-girl of his after his death, what is the way of her child? Al-Qasim twisted on him. A man of the people said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz decreed her child is in her status, freed with her freeing. Al-Qasim said: This is a view from him, and I do not see all she bears after tadbir; the mudabbarah and her child were from the third; if the master of the umm walad dies she is freed and he is freed; in this he is but just."
"16194","From al-Thawri, he said: When a man marries his umm walad or his mudabbarah, whatever child they bear is in her status; not sold, not gifted, not inherited. If the granter of tadbir dies she is freed, and freed is all she bore after tadbir. The mudabbarah and her child were from the third. If the master of the umm walad dies she is freed and her child is freed; whatever child she bore is in her status. After she bore from her master they are not sold, nor from the third, nor made to work for anything."
"16195","From Isma'il, from Ibn Awn, from al-Qasim and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, they said: The children of the mudabbarah are in their mother's status."
"16196","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata: Ibn Abbas, Ibn Umar, and others said: A man may approach his slave-girl if he grants her tadbir, if he wishes. Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard Ata say it."
"16197","Abd Allah ibn Umar reported, from Nafi: Ibn Umar granted tadbir to two slave-girls of his, and used to approach them, then freed one and married her to Nafi."
"16198","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, and from Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar granted tadbir to two slave-girls of his, and used to approach them until one of them was placed in tadbir."
"16199","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: No harm in a man approaching his mudabbarah, and he does not return in her."
"16200","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He disliked a man approaching his mudabbarah. He said: I said to him: Why do you dislike it? He said: For the word of Umar ibn al-Khattab: Do not approach her while anyone has a condition in her."
"16201","From Ibn Jurayj, from Umar: He freed a slave-girl of his after death, then approached her after that for seven years, then freed her while pregnant."
"16202","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: A man may approach his slave-girl mudabbarah, and not sell her, nor return in her."
"16203","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: Amr ibn al-As said: No harm in a man approaching his mudabbarah."
"16204","From Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: No harm in a man approaching his mudabbarah."
"16205","Umar ibn Hawshab reported, Isma'il ibn Umayyah informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: They had a boy called Tahman or Dhakwan, and his grandfather freed half of him. The slave came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You are freed in your freedom, and enslaved in your slavery. He served his master until he died. Isma'il said: The slave is only fully freed when one frees a slave of his half."
"16206","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who freed half a slave, he said: He is freed in his freedom, and enslaved in his slavery."
"16207","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from al-Hakam, from Ali: When half of him is freed, by account of what was freed, and he works. Al-Thawri said: And Hammad used to say that."
"16208","From al-Thawri, from Khalid ibn Salamah al-Fa'fa: A man came to Ibn Umar and said to him: I had a slave, I freed a third of him. Ibn Umar said: All of him is freed; Allah has no partner. Al-Thawri said: And we take by it."
"16209","From Ma'mar, from a man, he said: I was with al-Hasan when a man came to him and said: A woman has two slaves, she freed half of each of them so they may enter upon her. Al-Hasan said: Allah has no partner, Allah has no partner; they are both free."
"16210","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If he has a slave and frees a limb of him, all of him is freed; his inheritance is the inheritance of a free man, and his testimony is the testimony of a free man."
"16211","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When a man says to his slave: Your finger, or your nail, or a limb of you is free, all of him is freed."
"16212","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, the rest of him is valued in his wealth, if he has what reaches the slave's price. It is unknown whether his word: If he has what reaches the slave's price, is in the hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or something al-Zuhri said."
"16213","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, the slave is freed in his wealth if he has wealth."
"16214","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Umayyah informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, it is charged to the one who freed; its price is paid to his partners, and he is freed in the wealth of the one who freed."
"16215","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, the rest is freed in his wealth."
"16216","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Mijlaz: Two brothers of Juhaynah had a slave between them, and one freed his share, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) charged him until he sold livestock of his."
"16217","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Bashir ibn Nuhayk, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frees a share of his in a slave, the rest is freed in his wealth; if he has no wealth, the slave works."
"16218","From Ma'mar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, he said: A man freed a slave of his, having no wealth besides him, at his death, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed a third of him, and made him work for two thirds."
"16219","From Hushaim ibn Bashir, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha informed me, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Udhrah: A man of them freed at his death a boy of his, having no wealth, and it was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He freed a third of him, and ordered him to work for two thirds."
"16220","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When a slave is between them, and one frees his share, he guarantees if he has means; if he has no means, the slave works."
"16221","From al-Thawri, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, he heard Sulayman ibn Yasar say: When a man frees a portion in a slave, he guarantees the rest if he has wealth; if he has no wealth, the slave works for the rest. He said: I said to Sulayman: What if the slave is small? He said: So came the Sunnah."
"16222","From al-Thawri, from Hammad: He used to say: If he has of wealth the completion of his partner's share which he guaranteed for him, it is guaranteed, and no work is on the slave. If a dirham or more short of it, the slave works for half his price; no guarantee on the freer. If he freed while wealthy, and the judge did not judge until he went broke, he is guarantor, and nothing on the slave. If he freed while broke, and the judge did not judge until he became wealthy, work is on the slave. He said: And Hammad used to say: When he works, wala is between them. From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, Zakariyya, and Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Wala is for the one who... And Ibn Abi Layla said it; the word of Hammad is more beloved to me."
"16223","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: If a slave is between two men, and one frees his share without his partner's order, the rest of him is valued, then freed in the wealth of the one who freed, then this slave is made to work for what was charged in what was freed upon him of the slave. I said: Is the slave made to work for that whether insolvent or rich? He said: They claimed. He said: And I say: The slave is not made to work, except the freer be insolvent, then the slave is made to work then."
"16224","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and he said: The master does not follow the slave for what was charged upon him in his freeing."
"16225","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I wish to free from him what I free without his order, that he sit on his right of the slave, and the slave says: I will pay my value. He said: After he, and Amr ibn Dinar: Its master has more right to what remains, he sits on it if he wishes. He said: And I say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed he is freed, and no escape from that."
"16226","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Marthad said to me: Whoever frees a share of his upon his partners, while the slave is insolvent, and wants to take himself for its value, I see him most entitled to it if he pays."
"16227","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If the freer was insolvent, and the slave had wealth, and the one upon whom was freed said: I take the slave for that, but the slave refused. He said: Then the slave is not compelled then to anything of it, for himself a day, and for his master a day. He said: I said to Ata: If one of the partners contracts him, or settles with him by his partners' order, is it in the status of freeing? He said: Yes."
"16228","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubrumah: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to a man having a share in a slave: Do not spoil upon your partners, lest you guarantee."
"16229","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who freed a share of his in a slave, he said: He is valued the day he freed him."
"16230","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He was asked about a woman who said: If her husband marries, every slave of hers is free, then he married. He said: The foolish woman is not revoked in freeing; freeing is valid from every foolish woman and man, except she have a share in a slave, then none is freed until it is all hers."
"16231","From Ma'mar, from Abu Hamzah, from al-Nakha'i: A man freed a share of his in a slave, having orphan partners, and Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Wait for them until they grow up; if they wish to free, they free, and if they wish him to guarantee for them, he guarantees."
"16232","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: The family of Abu al-As had a boy they inherited, and freed him except a man of them. He sought intercession by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gifted him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he freed him. He used to say: I am the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16233","From Ma'mar, about a slave between two men, one freed his share, then the other freed after what he said: As for us we say: His wala and inheritance are between them. He said: I heard al-Zuhri and Amr ibn Dinar, about the slave between two men, one frees, then the other frees after, they said: Inheritance and wala are half between them, and no guarantee upon him."
"16234","He said: And Ibn Shubrumah said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to a man having a share in a slave: Do not spoil upon your partners, lest you guarantee."
"16235","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: Guarantee is on the first, and to him is inheritance and wala."
"16236","From al-Thawri, about a man who bought some of his brother from a man who owned the whole slave, he said: He is freed when he owns him, and the brother guarantees if wealthy, else the slave works. If it was inheritance he does not guarantee, because it came upon me unwillingly."
"16237","From al-Thawri, about a slave between two men, who bought half himself from one of them, he said: He is freed, and the seller of himself guarantees to his partner."
"16238","From al-Thawri, about a slave between two men, one sold his share to the slave's father, while the slave's father is insolvent, he said: If he wishes the seller guarantees, and if he wishes he charges the slave's father."
"16239","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When a slave frees a share of his in a slave, the rest is freed in his wealth; if he has no wealth, the slave works. He said: And when he works he is in the status of a slave, and his inheritance and wala are for the one for whom he works. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: His inheritance and wala are by portions, and Hammad said it."
"16240","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Habibah al-Ta'i, from Abu al-Darda, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The like of one who frees at death is like one who gives when sated."
"16241","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If there is a manumission and a bequest, begin with the manumission. From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Shurayh, the same as the saying of Ibrahim: Begin with the freeing."
"16242","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he said: Begin with the freeing. Al-Thawri and his companions said: Begin with the freeing."
"16243","From Ma'mar, from Qatada and Ata al-Khurasani, about a man who freed a third of his slave and bequeathed the rest of the third to people he named, they both said: Begin with the freeing, so the slave is freed completely, then if anything remains after his freeing it goes where he named."
"16244","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If the manumission and a bequest occur together, then by shares."
"16245","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he said: By shares."
"16246","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubruma, he said: The freeing stands as he named it, and his bequest is for whom he named, but the slave works to earn what remains due upon him."
"16247","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: The shortfall is thrown back upon the people of the manumission, and revision is made in the bequest. Amr ibn Dinar said it too, and both say: Begin with the freeing."
"16248","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, he said: A man died having freed six slaves, and he had no wealth besides them, so that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: If I had reached him, he would not have been buried with the Muslims. Then he cast lots among them, freed two, and kept four enslaved."
"16249","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, he said: A man freed slaves belonging to him, three in number, and he had no wealth besides them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots among them and freed one of them."
"16250","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Qays ibn Sa'd told me that he heard Makhul saying: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: A woman or a man freed six slaves of hers at the time of death, and she had no wealth besides them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was approached about that, and he cast lots among them. And Ata was listening, and he said: We used to say: They are made to work to earn."
"16251","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa told me, he said: I heard Makhul saying: An Ansari woman who died freed six slaves of hers, and she had no wealth. When that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he spoke a stern word about it, then he ordered six arrows and cast lots among them, and freed two. I said: From Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib? He said: He would only transmit it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Qays said to me: I bear witness to his transmission from Ibn al-Musayyib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sulayman said: But we do not act on that now, nor is judgment given by it with us, rather we make them work to earn the remaining two-thirds. He said: I kept debating with Makhul: if a slave was valued at a thousand dinars and the lot fell on him, so the wealth is gone? He said: We stop at the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said to Sulayman: The matter is straight upon what Makhul said. He said: Then how is valuation established? If the two who were freed exceed the third, it is taken from them, and if it falls short, what remains of the lot is also freed. And if there is surplus over anyone, it is taken from him. He said: Then it reached us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) valued them."
"16252","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: And Ata said: If he says: A third of my slaves are free, it is nothing until he names, so he says: So-and-so is free. But that was one who bequeaths a third of his slaves: so-and-so is free for so-and-so, a third of every slave. Or he bequeaths his slaves as inheritance, so let him take a third of every slave. He said: Then if he says: I free a third of my slaves, they are valued, then lots are cast among them, and a third of them is freed. Then if there is a shortfall, you take it from the one of the shortfall, the increase and the surplus."
"16253","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: A man bought a slave girl while he was sick, then freed her at his death. Those who had sold her came for her price, but found he had no wealth. They raised that to Ibn Mas'ud, and he said to her: Work to earn your price."
"16254","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim, he said: Ibn Mas'ud was asked about a man who freed his slave at death, having no wealth besides him, and with a debt upon him. He said: The slave works to earn his price."
"16255","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about one who has a debt upon him and has nothing but a slave, then frees him at his death. He wrote that the slave is sold and his debt is paid."
"16256","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: If a man frees a slave of his at death, having no wealth besides him, he is made to work to earn the two-thirds."
"16257","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, about a man who freed three slaves, having no wealth besides them, the price of one a thousand dinars, the second two thousand, and the third three thousand, he said: Cast lots among them. If the one worth a thousand comes out, cast lots between the other two, then take the surplus from whichever the lot falls on. If the one worth two thousand comes out, that is the third. If the one worth three thousand comes out, the surplus is taken from him."
"16258","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata and Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, about a man who freed a third of his slave at his death, they both said: What remains of the slave is valued against his third, so he is freed completely."
"16259","And I heard al-Thawri, or Hushaym, or one of them narrating, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, and from Yunus, from al-Hasan, they both said: If he frees a third of his slave at death, a third of him is freed, and the slave is made to work to earn the two-thirds, and the dead man bears no liability. Al-Thawri said: And the saying of Ata is what is relied upon."
"16260","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who frees a third of his slave at death. He said: What remains is valued against his third, then he is freed. I said: He has already bequeathed his third. He said: It is valued against his third, then he is freed, then the slave is made to work to earn. He said: And he said: If a sick man frees a third of his slave at death, he said: It is valued against his third. Then he named: A third of so-and-so is free as a bequest, then he died. His remaining two-thirds are valued against the testator out of his third, he is freed completely, and the price of his two-thirds goes free to the heir."
"16261","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim told me, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib came while I was sitting with him, with no one else with him, and he was asked: A man died leaving no wealth except a boy, then freed him. He said: He owns only a third of him. The slave is valued at his price, then made to work to earn the two-thirds. If he is unable, then for him is a day for himself, and for them two days."
"16262","Al-Thawri reported, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, he said: A man freed six slaves of his at his death, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots among them, and freed two of them."
"16263","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, about a man who freed six slaves of his at his death, he said: They are all valued, a third of them is freed, and they work to earn the two-thirds."
"16264","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, about a man who freed his slave at death, leaving a debt and no wealth, he said: The slave is made to work to earn his price."
"16265","From al-Aslami, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Ali, about a man who freed his slave at death, leaving a debt and no wealth, he said: The slave is made to work to earn his value. He said: And al-Hajjaj also told me, from al-Ala ibn Badr, from Abu Yahya al-A'raj, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"16266","From al-Thawri, about a man who has a mudabbar slave and a slave who is not mudabbar, and it was said to him: What are these two slaves? He said: One of them is free, then he died. Both slaves came, each claiming he is the free one, and he has no wealth besides them, the price of each three hundred dirhams. He said: As for the one who is not mudabbar, he works to earn a hundred and fifty. As for the mudabbar, he works for fifty."
"16267","From al-Thawri, about a man against whom two witnesses testified that he freed one of his two boys, without it being known which one, he said: Both are made to work to earn half, if you value them."
"16268","From al-Thawri, about a man who bequeathed that a mukatab slave of his be freed, and made other bequests, he said: If what is upon the mukatab is better for him, we count it for him. If the value is less, we count for him the value."
"16269","From al-Thawri, about a slave against whom two men testified that his master freed him, while his master has died, and they were asked: In his health or in his sickness? They said: We do not know. He said: He is from the third."
"16270","Al-Thawri said: About a woman who died leaving her sister and her husband, and she freed a boy worth five hundred, while upon her husband was seven hundred, and the husband is bankrupt. The sister said to the slave: You and I are but partners. You have only four hundred dirhams if the wealth comes out, for what was upon the husband is lost. And you give two hundred of the four that were yours in the third, and give fifty of the hundred that remained upon you, and you claim a hundred and fifty from the husband."
"16271","Sufyan said: About a man who freed two boys of his, the price of one four hundred and the price of the other two hundred, then the one worth four hundred died. The division is nine shares: for the heirs six hundred, and for the owner of the third three hundred. The owner of the four hundred died, so for him are two shares and for the owner of the two hundred one share. The heirs count with six shares and the owner of the debt with one share, so for him are seven hundred."
"16272","From al-Thawri, about a man who left four slaves, the value of each slave a hundred dinars, and he freed two of them, then one of them died after the death of his master. The arrows: for the dead one share, and what remains is upon five shares. For the freed one of that one share, and for the heirs four shares, and for the heirs five.... a hundred."
"16273","From al-Thawri, he said: About a slave contracted in writing for a thousand dirhams, then his master died and bequeathed fifty dirhams of his contract, and freed slaves, and made bequests. He said: The mukatab is not sold nor valued, and every person sells the mukatab for his share, and the mukatab counts with what was bequeathed to him along with them, except that beginning is with the freeing."
"16274","From Sufyan, about a man who died leaving a mukatab owing four hundred dirhams, and he freed a boy of his worth two hundred dirhams. He said: The slave who is not a mukatab gives them two-thirds of his value, and the mukatab slave sells for what he gave the heirs of the two-thirds of his value."
"16275","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, about a slave owned between two men, one of them testified against the other that he freed him, and the other denied. He said: If the one testified against is insolvent, the slave works to earn for him, and if he is solvent, he works to earn for both of them."
"16276","And I asked al-Thawri about it, and he said: Like the saying of Hammad. Ma'mar said: And I asked Ibn Shubruma, and he said: The slave is freed and there is no earning upon him."
"16277","From Muhammad ibn Umara, that he heard Abu Hanifa saying: If the one testified against is insolvent, the slave works to earn, and the wala is between them. If the one testified against is solvent, the wala of his half is suspended: if he admits that he freed, he deserves the wala, otherwise his wala is for the public treasury."
"16278","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab freed every Muslim among the slaves of the governorship, and stipulated: You shall serve the caliph after me for three years, and he shall keep you in the manner I used to keep you. He said: Then al-Khiyar bought his service of the three years from Uthman, for his boy Abu Farwa."
"16279","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab freed in his will everyone who had prayed two rak'as among the slaves of the public funds, and he freed slaves among the slaves of the public funds who used to dig graves for people, and stipulated upon them: You shall serve the caliph after me three years, and he shall keep you in the manner I used to keep you."
"16280","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman told me, he said: Nafi' told me, from Abd Allah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab freed everyone who prayed among the Arab captives, and made their freeing absolute, and stipulated upon them: You shall serve the caliph after me three years, and stipulated for them that he shall keep you in the like of what I used to keep you with. Then al-Khiyar bought his service of those three years from Uthman for Abu Farwa, and Uthman set al-Khiyar free, so he went, and Uthman took Abu Farwa."
"16281","From Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: That he freed a boy of his, and stipulated upon him that his work for three years belongs to him. He tended for him part of a year, then some of his relatives came to him, either in hajj or in umra, and Abd Allah said to him: I have left for you what I stipulated upon you. You are free, and there is no work upon you."
"16282","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no harm in the slave buying his service from his master for something he settles with him upon, as al-Khiyar did. Al-Thawri said about a man who says to his slave: Serve me ten years and you are free, then the master died before him. He said: He is a slave."
"16283","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ali had given in charity part of his land, making it charity after his death, and he freed slaves among his slaves, and stipulated upon them: You shall work in it five years."
"16284","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Ali gave in charity part of his land, making it charity after his death, and freed slaves among his slaves, and stipulated upon them: You shall work in that land five years."
"16285","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, or another, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: If he says: You are free, and he makes the freeing absolute, then every condition after it is void."
"16286","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Shubruma, he said: If a man says to his slave: You are free on condition you serve me ten years, then for him is his condition."
"16287","And I heard Abu Hanifa, asked about a man who said to his boy: If you pay me a hundred dinars then you are free. He said: So he paid them, then he is free, and his master takes the rest of his wealth."
"16288","From al-Thawri, about a man who freed his slave on condition he serve him ten years. He said: For him is his condition, if he agrees to that."
"16289","From al-Thawri, about a man who married his slave girl to a man, and the man stipulated upon him that whatever she bears from me is free. He said: For him is his condition, until her master sells her or dies, so she passes to another."
"16290","From Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A woman came to Shurayh and said: I freed this boy of mine on condition he pays me ten dirhams every month as long as I live. Shurayh said: Your freeing is valid, and your condition is void."
"16291","From al-Thawri, about a man who said to his slave girl: If you bear a boy then he is free. She bore a boy, then waited a while and bore another. He said: The first is freed."
"16292","From al-Thawri, he said: About a man who said to his slave girl: The first boy you bear is free. She bore him dead. It is nothing until she bears another belly. If she bears a boy then he is free. If he wishes he sells this one that has the condition. Freeing does not fall upon the dead."
"16293","And I heard Abu Hanifa, asked about a man who said: The first slave I own is free, then he owned two together. Hammad told me, from Ibrahim, he said: He frees whichever of them he wishes. Abu Hanifa said: And I say: Not one of them is freed, because they are not first."
"16294","From al-Thawri, about a man who said to a man: Free your slave and for you upon me a thousand dirhams. He said: We see his freeing valid, and there is nothing upon the one who ordered him. The wala is not for the one who freed, and the liability is upon the one the freed slave was ordered by, and his wealth is returned to him."
"16295","From al-Thawri, about a man who said to a man: Free your slave on my behalf, and he freed him on his behalf. He said: The wala belongs to the one who ordered. And he said: About a man whose mother said to him: Free your slave on my behalf, and he freed him on her behalf. He said: The wala belongs to her."
"16296","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: If a man says to a man: Free this boy of yours, and upon me is his price. He said: It is valid, and his wala belongs to his master as he freed him, and upon the guarantor is what he bore."
"16297","From al-Thawri, about a man who said to his slave: If I die suddenly then you are free, then the master was killed. He said: Killing is not sudden death. He is not freed."
"16298","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says to his slave: If you pay me a thousand dirhams then you are free, then it occurs to him not to accept anything from him, that belongs to the master. The like of it if he says: If this genealogist reviles then you are free, then it occurs to the master that there is nothing. He said: It is nothing. And if he says: You are free, and pay me such-and-such, then if the slave acknowledges and pays him, he is free. If he does not acknowledge paying him, then he is... there is nothing upon him."
"16299","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man frees his slave girl and excepts what is in her womb, then for him is what he excepted. Sufyan said: And we do not take by that. We say: If he excepts what is in her womb, all of her is freed. Her child is but like a limb of her. And if he frees what is in her womb and does not free her, nothing is freed except what is in her womb."
"16300","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they both said: About a man who freed a pregnant slave girl of his, and excepted what is in her womb. They both said: That is nothing. She and her child are both free. Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, and al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, they both said: His condition is valid, like the saying of Ibrahim. From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hakam ibn Utayba and al-Hasan told me, they both say: She and her child are both free. From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of that."
"16301","Ma'mar reported, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked him about a man who bought his son while he was sick, then the father died of that sickness of his. He said: If the son comes out of the third, he inherits his father. If he does not come out of the third, he works to earn and does not inherit."
"16302","From Sufyan, about a man who said to his slave: The day I sell you, you are free. He said: It is nothing, and he is his slave. And whoever says: When I sell you, you are free, it is the same. Sufyan said: Its meaning is only when I do that. He said: And the like of that is one who says: The day I die, you are free, then he dies by night or day, so he is free."
"16303","From Sufyan, about a man who says to his slave: He is free on the day he sells him. He said: Ibn Abi Layla and Ibn Shubruma used to pause that upon him. Sufyan said: And we do not see it as anything."
"16304","From Sufyan, about a man who swore by the freeing of his slave: If I part from you or you part from me. He said: If he said I parted from you, then upon him... if it was upon him then there is nothing upon him. If he said you parted from me, then upon him the slave, so he is free."
"16305","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, about a slave who slipped wealth to a man, so he bought him and freed him. He said: The sale and the freeing are valid, and his master takes from the buyer the price for which he bought him, and the wala belongs to the one who freed."
"16306","From Sufyan, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, about a man who sells his slave to people, and stipulates upon them that they free him, and says to his slave: That you give such-and-such. He said: There is nothing upon the slave."
"16307","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: No judgment is given against the slave for anything, except that he is scrupulous and gives it."
"16308","From Abu Sufyan, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, about a man whose slave gave him wealth, so he bought him and freed him. He said: If I had seized him I would have punished him a severe punishment."
"16309","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib struck the face of his slave girl, then he came with her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glory be to Allah! What carried you to this? He said: O Messenger of Allah, if I knew she was a believer I would free her. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her, then said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"16310","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from a man of the Ansar who came with a black slave girl to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah, upon me is a believing neck. If you see this one a believer... Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah? She said: Yes. He said: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: Yes. He said: Do you believe in the resurrection after death? She said: Yes. He said: Free her."
"16311","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me: That a man had a slave girl with sheep she pastured, and there was a prized ewe, meaning abundant in milk, among those sheep of his. He wanted to give her to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then a wolf came and tore her udder. The man became angry and struck the face of his slave girl. He came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and mentioned that a complete believing neck was upon him, which he had intended to make her when he struck her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Bring her to me! Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah? She said: Yes. And that Muhammad is the slave of Allah and His Messenger? She said: Yes. And that death and resurrection are true? She said: Yes. And that Paradise and Hell are true? She said: Yes. When he finished he said: Free or keep? I said: Is this established? He said: Yes. And they claimed, and Abu al-Zubayr narrated it to me, then she gave birth thereafter among Quraysh."
"16312","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: A man struck a slave girl of his, then he came with her to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consulting him about freeing her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Where is your Lord? She pointed to the sky. He said: Who am I? She said: You are the Messenger of Allah. He said: I think he also mentioned the resurrection after death, and Paradise, and Hell, then he said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"16313","Ma'mar reported, and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Abu Marawih al-Ghifari, from Abu Dharr, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which necks are most excellent? He said: The most precious of them to their owners, and the most excellent, and the highest in price."
"16314","From al-Thawri, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, that a man asked him about a child of zina and a child of legitimate birth, which of them is freed? He said: Look at the higher of the two in price."
"16315","From al-Thawri, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qurashi, that Ibn Abbas was asked about a child of zina and a child of legitimate birth, and he said: Look at the higher of the two in price. From al-Thawri, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: He used to regard the child of zina like any other."
"16316","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, they both said: The child of zina is valid for the obligatory neck, because every newborn is born upon the fitra."
"16317","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The child of zina does not suffice for the obligatory neck."
"16318","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Neither the child of a prostitute, nor a mother of a child, nor a mudabbar, nor a Jew, nor a Christian, nor a polytheist suffices for an obligatory neck. He said: And I do not know al-Zuhri but said: The mukatab suffices for the obligatory neck."
"16319","From Ma'mar, from Abu Umar al-Madani, he said: Ibn Umar asked us about daytime recitation, then he stood praying, and perhaps he let us hear the verse. He said: Then he went out to the market and we walked with him, and he would not pass a young or old one but Ibn Umar greeted him, until he came to the noon market with his staff in his hand, and he began to poke with his staff in the side of the camel, then say: For how much is this? He said: Then he haggled with another. He said: Then a man came to him and said: A neck was upon me, then I bought it from a man as a neck, then freed it. Then I was told that its owner had picked it up as a foundling. Ibn Umar said: Allah has not accepted your neck from you. Go and take your paper. He said: But I have freed her. He said: I have commanded you. It is that. It does not suffice for you."
"16320","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: A small child of zina, does he suffice for a believing neck, if he has not reached the age of accountability? He said: No, but a grown man who was truthful."
"16321","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: The mother of a child and the mudabbara suffice as a neck."
"16322","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: The mother of a child and the mudabbara suffice as a neck. And Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, the like of that."
"16323","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever frees out of work, it suffices, if he says: If he was doing work and freed, it suffices if it has benefit for him."
"16324","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: Neither a cripple, nor one with no hands, nor a leper, nor one of great affliction suffices for the obligatory neck. And the like of this."
"16325","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Killing a soul by mistake. He said: Nothing is valid but a believing neck, as Allah, mighty and majestic, said. Ata said: If a man says to his boy: He is free, he is not free until he says: For Allah. Perhaps he did not intend the freeing."
"16326","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: A suckling infant is not valid for accidental killing, except one who prayed. For in the reading of Ubayy ibn Ka'b: Then the freeing of a believing neck. An infant is not valid in it."
"16327","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is a believing neck that is not sound valid for killing a soul by mistake, while he benefits by it, lame, and crippled in the hand? He deemed the sound one lawful, and mentioned sacrificial camels."
"16328","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A suckling infant is valid for zihar."
"16329","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The oath in zihar, for he did not mention believing, does a non-believing neck suffice? He said: We see in it nothing but believing. And Amr ibn Dinar said it."
"16330","Al-Thawri reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: The Jew and the Christian suffice for zihar and oaths."
"16331","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The believing neck, is an infant valid in it? He said: Yes. I said: But how, when he has not prayed, and I do not know whether he is a Muslim or not? He said that. I reviewed it with him after days, and he said: I see him but a Muslim. He said: And he said: Amr ibn Dinar said: I see him but one who has reached maturity and submitted. I said to Ata: And the one who reached maturity, his religion is the religion of a Muslim? He said: Yes! And prayer is performed over him. I said to Ata: Then he was taken captive as a non-Arab who had not reached the age of accountability. He said: Whoever was born here is more beloved to him, and perhaps he will decree."
"16332","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you like that the suckling be delayed two or three years until it is known that he is sound? He said: Yes."
"16333","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: The one-eyed suffices for the neck."
"16334","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The blind is valid as a neck."
"16335","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Then the squint-eyed? He said: The squint-eyed is lighter than the lame, so it discharges, and the sound is more beloved to me. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: I see the one-eyed and the crippled in the hand valid, if safe."
